Australia,AUS,1. Is the prohibition of non-intervention a norm of customary international law?,Yes,¶7. Harmful conduct in cyberspace that does not constitute a use of force may still constitute a breach of the duty not to intervene in the internal or external affairs of another State. This obligation is encapsulated in Article 2(7) of the Charter and in customary international law.,UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Australia,AUS,2. Is the scope of the principle of non-intervention uncertain?,Silent,,,,
Australia,AUS,3. Does the prohibition of non-intervention apply to cyber-operations?,Yes,¶7. Harmful conduct in cyberspace that does not constitute a use of force may still constitute a breach of the duty not to intervene in the internal or external affairs of another State.,UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Australia,AUS,4. Is coercion a constituent element of prohibited intervention?,Yes,"¶8. A prohibited intervention is one that interferes by coercive means, either directly or indirectly, in matters that a State is permitted by the principle of State sovereignty to decide freely.",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Australia,AUS,4.1 Is coercion limited to the employment of physical force?,No,"¶8. Coercive means are those that effectively deprive the State of the ability to control, decide upon or govern matters of an inherently sovereign nature. ¶9. The use by a State of cyber activities to prevent another State from holding an election, or manipulate the electoral system to alter the results of an election in another State, intervene in the fundamental operation of Parliament, or significantly disrupt the functioning of a States’ financial systems would constitute a violation of  the principle of non-intervention.",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Australia,AUS,4.2 Can dictatorial demands or acts of compulsion or imposition constitute coercion?,Silent,,,,
Australia,AUS,4.3 Does the deprivation of a State’s ability to control matters within its domaine réservé amount to coercion?,Yes,"¶8. Coercive means are those that effectively deprive the State of the ability to control, decide upon or govern matters of an inherently sovereign nature.
 ¶9. The use by a State of cyber activities to prevent another State from holding an election, or manipulate the electoral system to alter the results of an election in another State, intervene in the fundamental operation of Parliament, or significantly disrupt the functioning of a States’ financial systems would constitute a violation of  the principle of non-intervention.",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Australia,AUS,4.4 Does coercion extend to any affirmative acts designed to deprive another state of its freedom of choice with regard to a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?,Yes,"¶8. Coercive means are those that effectively deprive the State of the ability to control, decide upon or govern matters of an inherently sovereign nature. ¶9. The use by a State of cyber activities to prevent another State from holding an election, or manipulate the electoral system to alter the results of an election in another State, intervene in the fundamental operation of Parliament, or significantly disrupt the functioning of a States’ financial systems would constitute a violation of  the principle of non-intervention.",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Australia,AUS,4.5 Must there be a causal nexus between the responsible state’s actions/efforts and the effects on the target state’s internal or external affairs?,Silent,,,,
Australia,AUS,4.6  Can attempts to coerce an outcome amount to prohibited intervention?,Yes,"¶8. A prohibited intervention is one that interferes by coercive means, either directly or indirectly, in matters that a State is permitted by the principle of State sovereignty to decide freely. Such matters include a State’s economic, political, social and cultural systems and foreign policy. Coercive means are those that effectively deprive or are intended to deprive the State of the ability to control, decide upon or govern matters of an inherently sovereign nature.",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Australia,AUS,4.7 Is intent a constituent element of coercion?,Ambiguous,"¶8. A prohibited intervention is one that interferes by coercive means, either directly or indirectly, in matters that a State is permitted by the principle of State sovereignty to decide freely. Such matters include a State’s economic, political, social and cultural systems and foreign policy. Coercive means are those that effectively deprive or are intended to deprive the State of the ability to control, decide upon or govern matters of an inherently sovereign nature.",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Australia,AUS,"4.8 Must the action(s) constituting coercion be designed/intended to influence outcomes in, or conduct with respect to, a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?",Ambiguous,"¶8. Coercive means are those that effectively deprive the State of the ability to control, decide upon or govern matters of an inherently sovereign nature. ¶9. The use by a State of cyber activities to prevent another State from holding an election, or manipulate the electoral system to alter the results of an election in another State, intervene in the fundamental operation of Parliament, or significantly disrupt the functioning of a States’ financial systems would constitute a violation of  the principle of non-intervention.",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Australia,AUS,4.9  Can coercion be indirect?,Yes,"¶8. A prohibited intervention is one that interferes by coercive means, either directly or indirectly, in matters that a State is permitted by the principle of State sovereignty to decide freely.",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
