Austria,AUT,1. Is the prohibition of non-intervention a norm of customary international law?,Yes,¶1. The customary international law rule of non-intervention prohibits the coercive interference in the internal or external affairs (‘domaine réservé’) of another state.”,Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law,https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,
Austria,AUT,2. Is the scope of the principle of non-intervention uncertain?,Silent,,,,
Austria,AUT,3. Does the prohibition of non-intervention apply to cyber-operations?,Yes,"The customary international law rule prohibiting intervention, i.e. coercive interference, by one state in the internal or external affairs of another state also applies to cyber activities.",Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law,https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,
Austria,AUT,4. Is coercion a constituent element of prohibited intervention?,Yes,"The customary international law rule prohibiting intervention, i.e. coercive interference, by one state in the internal or external affairs of another state also applies to cyber activities ¶1. The customary international law rule of non-intervention prohibits the coercive interference in the internal or external affairs (‘domaine réservé’) of another state. ¶3. Coercion occurs when a state seeks to compel another state to change its behaviour with respect to its internal or external affairs.",Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law,https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,
Austria,AUT,4.1 Is coercion limited to the employment of physical force?,No,"¶3. Coercion is most evident in an intervention using force. However, also non-forcible forms of interference can be coercive.",Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law,https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,
Austria,AUT,4.2 Can dictatorial demands or acts of compulsion or imposition constitute coercion?,Yes,¶3. Coercion occurs when a state seeks to compel another state to change its behaviour... i.e. to force that state to act in an involuntary manner or involuntarily refrain from acting in a particular way.,Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law,https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,
Austria,AUT,4.3 Does the deprivation of a State’s ability to control matters within its domaine réservé amount to coercion?,Ambiguous,¶3. Coercion occurs when a state seeks to compel another state to change its behaviour with respect to its internal or external affairs.,Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law,https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,
Austria,AUT,4.4 Does coercion extend to any affirmative acts designed to deprive another state of its freedom of choice with regard to a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?,Yes,"¶3. Coercion occurs when a state seeks to compel another state to change its behaviour with respect to its internal or external affairs, i.e. to force that state to act in an involuntary manner or involuntarily refrain from acting in a particular way.",Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law,https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,
Austria,AUT,4.5 Must there be a causal nexus between the responsible state’s actions/efforts and the effects on the target state’s internal or external affairs?,Silent,,,,
Austria,AUT,4.6 Can attempts to coerce an outcome amount to prohibited intervention?,Yes,"¶3. Coercion occurs when a state seeks to compel another state to change its behaviour with respect to its internal or external affairs, i.e. to force that state to act in an involuntary manner or involuntarily  refrain  from  acting  in  a  particular  way...For instance, a cyber activity that interferes with a state’s ability to hold elections or which manipulates election results could, if undertaken to compel a state to involuntarily  change  a  government  policy,  constitute  a  violation  of  the  prohibition  of intervention.",Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law,https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,
Austria,AUT,4.7 Is intent a constituent element of coercion?,Ambiguous,"¶3. Coercion occurs when a state seeks to compel another state to change its behaviour with respect to its internal or external affairs, i.e. to force that state to act in an involuntary manner or  involuntarily  refrain  from  acting  in  a  particular  way...For instance, a cyber activity that interferes with a state’s ability to hold elections or which manipulates election results could, if undertaken to compel a state to involuntarily  change  a  government  policy,  constitute  a  violation  of  the  prohibition  of intervention.",Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law,https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,
Austria,AUT,"4.8 Must the action(s) constituting coercion be designed/intended to influence outcomes in, or conduct with respect to, a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?",Yes,"¶3. Coercion occurs when a state seeks to compel another state to change its behaviour with respect to its internal or external affairs, i.e. to force that state to act in an involuntary manner or involuntarily refrain  from  acting in a  particular way...For instance, a cyber activity that interferes with a state’s ability to hold elections or which manipulates election results could, if undertaken to compel a state to involuntarily change  a  government policy, constitute  a  violation  of  the  prohibition  of intervention.",Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law,https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,
Austria,AUT,4.9   Can coercion be indirect?,Silent,,,,
Austria,AUT,4.10 Must the target state have knowledge of the coercive cyber operation constituting the purported intervention?,Silent,,,,
Austria,AUT,5. Does intervention include forms of persuasion or propaganda?,Yes,"¶3. For instance, a cyber activity that interferes with a state’s ability to hold elections or which manipulates election results could, if undertaken to compel a state to involuntarily  change  a  government  policy,  constitute  a  violation  of  the  prohibition  of intervention. Large-scale cyber activities, including disinformation campaigns, conducted by or attributable to a state may also constitute... a violation of the prohibition of intervention.",Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law,https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,
Austria,AUT,6. Is there a de minimis exception to the prohibition?,Silent,,,,
Austria,AUT,"7. Must the intervention be directed at matters over which the victim state is permitted by the principle of sovereignty, to decide freely?",Yes,"¶1. The customary international law rule of non-intervention prohibits the coercive interference in the internal or external affairs (“domaine réservé”) of another state. It was affirmed in various declarations of the UNGA, including the Friendly Relations Declaration.  ¶2. As was held by the International Court of Justice (ICJ) in the Nicaragua case, the internal or external affairs of a state include matters in which each state is to decide freely, such as its “choice of a political, economic, social and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy.",Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law,https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,
Austria,AUT,7.1 Does the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives include only internal affairs?,No,"¶2. As was held by the International Court of Justice (ICJ) in the Nicaragua case, the internal or external affairs of a state include matters in which each state is to decide freely, such as its choice of a political, economic, social and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy.",Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law,https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,
Austria,AUT,7.2 Does the prohibition proscribe intervention in a state’s external (foreign) affairs?,Yes,"¶2. As was held by the International Court of Justice (ICJ) in the Nicaragua case, the internal or external affairs of a state include matters in which each state is to decide freely, such as its choice of a political, economic, social and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy.",Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law,https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,
Austria,AUT,7.3 Is the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives referred to as domaine réservé?,Yes,¶1. The customary international law rule of non-intervention prohibits the coercive interference in the internal or external affairs (“domaine réservé”) of another state.,Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law,https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,
Austria,AUT,"7.4 Is the domaine réservé limited to matters involving the state’s choice of a political, economic, social, and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy?",Ambiguous,"¶2. The internal or external affairs of a state include matters in which each state is to decide freely, such as its “choice of a political, economic, social and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy.”",Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law,https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,
Austria,AUT,7.5 Does international law regulation over a matter of sovereign prerogative remove it from the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Austria,AUT,7.6 Is critical infrastructure part of the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Austria,AUT,7.7 Are elections part of the domaine réservé?,Yes,"¶3. For instance, a cyber activity that interferes with a state’s ability to hold elections or which manipulates election results could, if undertaken to compel a state to involuntarily change a government policy, constitute a violation of the prohibition of intervention.",Position Paper of the Republic of Austria: Cyber Activities and International Law,https://perma.cc/J6LL-59E9,
Austria,AUT,7.8 Is the operation of legislatures a part of the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
