Belgium,BEL,1. Is the prohibition of non-intervention a norm of customary international law?,Yes,"¶1. A cyber operation could be considered as a violation of the principle of nonintervention in the internal affairs of a State and therefore constitute an internationally wrongful act.  ¶2. This principle of non-intervention is customary in nature and its constituent elements have been defined by the International Court of Justice, in the Nicaragua case, as follows...","The National Position of Belgium on International Law Applicable to Cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,2. Is the scope of the principle of non-intervention uncertain?,No,"¶2. This principle of non-intervention is customary in nature and its constituent elements have been defined by the International Court of Justice, in the Nicaragua case, as follows: “(...) the principle forbids all States or groups of States to intervene directly or indirectly in internal or external affairs of other States. A prohibited intervention must accordingly be one bearing on matters in which each State is permitted, by the principle of State sovereignty, to decide freely. One of these is the choice of a political, economic, social and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy. Intervention is wrongful when it uses methods of coercion in regard to such choices, which must remain free ones. "" There are therefore three elements that must be met for a cyber operation to be classified as an intervention in the internal affairs of a State.","The National Position of Belgium on International Law Applicable to Cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,3. Does the prohibition of non-intervention apply to cyber-operations?,Yes,"¶3. As an example, and provided that the level of coercion is considered sufficient, a cyber operation, aimed at influencing or tampering with electoral results within a State, where such an operation could undermine or be likely to undermine the country's political, economic or social system, could constitute an intervention in the internal affairs of a State, and therefore a violation of the principle of non-intervention.","The National Position of Belgium on International Law Applicable to Cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,4. Is coercion a constituent element of prohibited intervention?,Yes,"¶2. There are therefore three elements that must be met for a cyber operation to be classified as an intervention in the internal affairs of a State:  the intervention must concern matters on which a State can decide freely (the term 'reserved area' is also used; both the internal and external affairs of a State are covered here),  the use of coercion and  a causal link between the coercive act and the effects on the reserved area of the targeted State.","The National Position of Belgium on International Law Applicable to Cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,4.1 Is coercion limited to the employment of physical force?,Silent,,,,
Belgium,BEL,4.2 Can dictatorial demands or acts of compulsion or imposition constitute coercion?,Silent,,,,
Belgium,BEL,4.3 Does the deprivation of a State’s ability to control matters within its domaine réservé amount to coercion?,Ambiguous,"¶2. There are therefore three elements that must be met for a cyber operation to be classified as an intervention in the internal affairs of a State:  the intervention must concern matters on which a State can decide freely (the term 'reserved area' is also used; both the internal and external affairs of a State are covered here),  the use of coercion and  a causal link between the coercive act and the effects on the reserved area of the targeted State.","The National Position of Belgium on International Law Applicable to Cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,4.4 Does coercion extend to any affirmative acts designed to deprive another state of its freedom of choice with regard to a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?,Silent,,,,
Belgium,BEL,4.5 Must there be a causal nexus between the responsible state’s actions/efforts and the effects on the target state’s internal or external affairs?,Yes,"¶2. There are therefore three elements that must be met for a cyber operation to be classified as an intervention in the internal affairs of a State:  the intervention must concern matters on which a State can decide freely (the term 'reserved area' is also used; both the internal and external affairs of a State are covered here),  the use of coercion and  a causal link between the coercive act and the effects on the reserved area of the targeted State.","The National Position of Belgium on International Law Applicable to Cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,4.6 Can attempts to coerce an outcome amount to prohibited intervention?,Yes,"As an example, and provided that the level of coercion is considered sufficient, a cyber operation, aimed at influencing or tampering with electoral results within a State, where such an operation could undermine or be likely to undermine the country's political, economic or social system, could constitute an intervention in the internal affairs of a State, and therefore a violation of the principle of non-intervention.","The national position of Belgium on international law applicable to cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,4.7 Is intent a constituent element of coercion?,Silent,,,,
Belgium,BEL,"4.8 Must the action(s) constituting coercion be designed/intended to influence outcomes in, or conduct with respect to, a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?",Silent,,,,
Belgium,BEL,4.9  Can coercion be indirect?,Silent,,,,
Belgium,BEL,4.10 Must the target state have knowledge of the coercive cyber operation constituting the purported intervention?,Silent,,,,
Belgium,BEL,5. Does intervention include forms of persuasion or propaganda?,Silent,,,,
Belgium,BEL,6. Is there a de minimis exception to the prohibition?,Silent,,,,
Belgium,BEL,"7. Must the intervention be directed at matters over which the victim state is permitted by the principle of sovereignty, to decide freely?",Yes,"¶2. There are therefore three elements that must be met for a cyber operation to be classified as an intervention in the internal affairs of a State:  the intervention must concern matters on which a State can decide freely (the term 'reserved area' is also used; both the internal and external affairs of a State are covered here), the use of coercion and  a causal link between the coercive act and the effects on the reserved area of the targeted State.","The National Position of Belgium on International Law Applicable to Cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,7.1 Does the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives include only internal affairs?,No,"¶2. There are therefore three elements that must be met for a cyber operation to be classified as an intervention in the internal affairs of a State:  the intervention must concern matters on which a State can decide freely (the term 'reserved area' is also used; both the internal and external affairs of a State are covered here),  the use of coercion and  a causal link between the coercive act and the effects on the reserved area of the targeted State.","The National Position of Belgium on International Law Applicable to Cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,7.2 Does the prohibition proscribe intervention in a state’s external (foreign) affairs?,Yes,"¶2. There are therefore three elements that must be met for a cyber operation to be classified as an intervention in the internal affairs of a State:  the intervention must concern matters on which a State can decide freely (the term 'reserved area' is also used; both the internal and external affairs of a State are covered here),  the use of coercion and  a causal link between the coercive act and the effects on the reserved area of the targeted State.","The National Position of Belgium on International Law Applicable to Cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,7.3 Is the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives referred to as domaine réservé?,Yes,"¶2. There are therefore three elements that must be met for a cyber operation to be classified as an intervention in the internal affairs of a State: the intervention must concern matters on which a State can decide freely (the term 'reserved area' is also used; both the internal and external affairs of a State are covered here),  the use of coercion and  a causal link between the coercive act and the effects on the reserved area of the targeted State.","The National Position of Belgium on International Law Applicable to Cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,"7.4 Is the domaine réservé limited to matters involving the state’s choice of a political, economic, social, and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy?",No,"¶2. This principle of non-intervention is customary in nature and its constituent elements have been defined by the International Court of Justice, in the Nicaragua case, as follows: “(...) the principle forbids all States or groups of States to intervene directly or indirectly in internal or external affairs of other States. A prohibited intervention must accordingly be one bearing on matters in which each State is permitted, by the principle of State sovereignty, to decide freely. One of these is the choice of a political, economic, social and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy. Intervention is wrongful when it uses methods of coercion in regard to such choices, which must remain free ones.”","The National Position of Belgium on International Law Applicable to Cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
Belgium,BEL,7.5 Does international law regulation over a matter of sovereign prerogative remove it from the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Belgium,BEL,7.6 Is critical infrastructure part of the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Belgium,BEL,7.7 Are elections part of the domaine réservé?,Yes,"¶3. As an example, and provided that the level of coercion is considered sufficient, a cyber operation, aimed at influencing or tampering with electoral results within a State, where such an operation could undermine or be likely to undermine the country's political, economic or social system, could constitute an intervention in the internal affairs of a State, and therefore a violation of the principle of non-intervention.","The National Position of Belgium on International Law Applicable to Cyberspace,",https://docs-library.unoda.org/Open-Ended_Working_Group_on_Information_and_Communication_Technologies_-_(2021)/National_position_BE_on_international_law_DEF_171025.pdf,
