Brazil,BRA,1. Is the prohibition of non-intervention a norm of customary international law?,Yes,"¶1. The principle of non-intervention, which is considered customary international law, refers to “the right of every sovereign State to conduct its affairs without outside interference”","Brazil, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Brazil,BRA,2. Is the scope of the principle of non-intervention uncertain?,Silent,,,,
Brazil,BRA,3. Does the prohibition of non-intervention apply to cyber-operations?,Yes,¶1....the customary norm prohibiting intervention in the internal affairs of another State applies irrespective of the means or medium used and extends to the use of ICTs by States.,"Brazil, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Brazil,BRA,4. Is coercion a constituent element of prohibited intervention?,Yes,"¶2. To violate the principle of non-intervention, the malicious use of ICTs against another State must involve an element of coercion affecting the right of the victim State to freely choose its political, economic, social and cultural system, and to formulate its foreign policy.","Brazil, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Brazil,BRA,4.1 Is coercion limited to the employment of physical force?,Ambiguous,"¶2. To violate the principle of non-intervention, the malicious use of ICTs against another State must involve an element of coercion affecting the right of the victim State to freely choose its political, economic, social and cultural system, and to formulate its foreign policy.","Brazil, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Brazil,BRA,4.2 Can dictatorial demands or acts of compulsion or imposition constitute coercion?,Silent,,,,
Brazil,BRA,4.3 Does the deprivation of a State’s ability to control matters within its domaine réservé amount to coercion?,Ambiguous,"¶2. To violate the principle of non-intervention, the malicious use of ICTs against another State must involve an element of coercion affecting the right of the victim State to freely choose its political, economic, social and cultural system, and to formulate its foreign policy. If attributable to a State, this breach entails this State’s international responsibility.","Brazil, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Brazil,BRA,4.4 Does coercion extend to any affirmative acts designed to deprive another state of its freedom of choice with regard to a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?,Yes,"¶2. To violate the principle of non-intervention, the malicious use of ICTs against another State must involve an element of coercion affecting the right of the victim State to freely choose its political, economic, social and cultural system, and to formulate its foreign policy. If attributable to a State, this breach entails this State’s international responsibility.","Brazil, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Brazil,BRA,4.5 Must there be a causal nexus between the responsible state’s actions/efforts and the effects on the target state’s internal or external affairs?,Silent,,,,
Brazil,BRA,4.6 Can attempts to coerce an outcome amount to prohibited intervention?,Silent,,,,
Brazil,BRA,4.7 Is intent a constituent element of coercion?,Silent,,,,
Brazil,BRA,"4.8 Must the action(s) constituting coercion be designed/intended to influence outcomes in, or conduct with respect to, a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?",Silent,,,,
Brazil,BRA,4.9 Can coercion be indirect?,Silent,,,,
Brazil,BRA,4.10 Must the target state have knowledge of the coercive cyber operation constituting the purported intervention?,Silent,,,,
Brazil,BRA,5. Does intervention include forms of persuasion or propaganda?,Silent,,,,
Brazil,BRA,6. Is there a de minimis exception to the prohibition?,Silent,,,,
Brazil,BRA,"7. Must the intervention be directed at matters over which the victim state is permitted by the principle of sovereignty, to decide freely?",Yes,"¶2....the malicious use of ICTs... must involve an element of coercion affecting the right of the victim State to freely choose its political, economic, social and cultural system, and to formulate its foreign policy.","Brazil, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Brazil,BRA,7.1 Does the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives include only internal affairs?,No,"¶2. To violate the principle of non-intervention, the malicious use of ICTs against another State must involve an element of coercion affecting the right of the victim State to freely choose its political, economic, social and cultural system, and to formulate its foreign policy. If attributable to a State, this breach entails this State’s international responsibility.","Brazil, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Brazil,BRA,7.2 Does the prohibition proscribe intervention in a state’s external (foreign) affairs?,Yes,"¶2. To violate the principle of non-intervention, the malicious use of ICTs against another State must involve an element of coercion affecting the right of the victim State to freely choose its political, economic, social and cultural system, and to formulate its foreign policy. If attributable to a State, this breach entails this State’s international responsibility.","Brazil, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Brazil,BRA,7.3 Is the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives referred to as domaine réservé?,No,,,,
Brazil,BRA,"7.4 Is the domaine réservé limited to matters involving the state’s choice of a political, economic, social, and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy?",Silent,,,,
Brazil,BRA,7.5 Does international law regulation over a matter of sovereign prerogative remove it from the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Brazil,BRA,7.6 Is critical infrastructure part of the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Brazil,BRA,"7.7 Are elections part of the domaine réservé, internal and/or external affairs?",Yes,"¶3. Considering that elections are at the core of a State’s internal affairs, should the malicious use of ICTs... involve some level of coercion, then it must be prohibited by the principle of non-intervention.","Brazil, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Brazil,BRA,7.8 Is the operation of legislatures a part of the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Brazil,BRA,7.9 Is financial system stability part of the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Brazil,BRA,7.10 Are medical services part of the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Canada,CAN,1. Is the prohibition of non-intervention a norm of customary international law?,Yes,¶22. State cyber activities may breach the foundational international law prohibition of intervention in the internal or external affairs of another State.,Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,
Canada,CAN,2. Is the scope of the principle of non-intervention uncertain?,Yes,"¶25. Whether or not a cyber activity meets the threshold for a violation of the rule on territorial sovereignty or rises to the level of a violation of the rule against intervention will be determined on a case-by-case basis. As with the thresholds for violations of territorial sovereignty, Canada believes that further State practice and opinio juris will help clarify the thresholds for the rule of non-intervention, and the scope of customary law in this area over time.",Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,
Canada,CAN,3. Does the prohibition of non-intervention apply to cyber-operations?,Yes,"¶22. State cyber activities may breach the foundational international law prohibition of intervention in the internal or external affairs of another State. This would be the case where both of the following conditions are met: the activities aim to interfere with the internal or external affairs of the affected State involving its inherently sovereign functions, known as domaine réservé;  and the activities would cause coercive effects that deprive, compel, or impose an outcome on the affected State on matters in which it has free choice.",Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,
Canada,CAN,4. Is coercion a constituent element of prohibited intervention?,Yes,"¶22. State cyber activities may breach the foundational international law prohibition of intervention in the internal or external affairs of another State. This would be the case where both of the following conditions are met:the activities aim to interfere with the internal or external affairs of the affected State involving its inherently sovereign functions, known as domaine réservé;  and the activities would cause coercive effects that deprive, compel, or impose an outcome on the affected State on matters in which it has free choice.",Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,
Canada,CAN,4.1 Is coercion limited to the employment of physical force?,No,"¶23. In its most serious form, coercion may arise through the threat or use of force but could also arise where a cyber activity is designed to deprive the affected State of its freedom of choice. Coercion must be distinguished from other conduct such as public diplomacy, criticism, persuasion, and propaganda.",Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,
Canada,CAN,4.2 Can dictatorial demands or acts of compulsion or imposition constitute coercion?,Yes,"¶22. State cyber activities may breach the foundational international law prohibition of intervention in the internal or external affairs of another State. This would be the case where both of the following conditions are met:... and, The activities would cause coercive effects that deprive, compel, or impose an outcome on the affected State.",Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,
Canada,CAN,4.3 Does the deprivation of a State’s ability to control matters within its domaine réservé amount to coercion?,Silent,,,,
Canada,CAN,4.4 Does coercion extend to any affirmative acts designed to deprive another state of its freedom of choice with regard to a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?,Ambiguous,"¶23. In its most serious form, coercion may arise through the threat or use of force but could also arise where a cyber activity is designed to deprive the affected State of its freedom of choice.",Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,
Canada,CAN,4.5 Must there be a causal nexus between the responsible state’s actions/efforts and the effects on the target state’s internal or external affairs?,Yes,"¶22. State cyber activities may breach the foundational international law prohibition of intervention in the internal or external affairs of another State. This would be the case where both of the following conditions are met: the activities aim to interfere with the internal or external affairs of the affected State involving its inherently sovereign functions, known as domaine réservé;  and the activities would cause coercive effects that deprive, compel, or impose an outcome on the affected State on matters in which it has free choice.",Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,
Canada,CAN,4.6 Can attempts to coerce an outcome amount to prohibited intervention?,No,"¶22....activities would cause coercive effects that deprive, compel, or impose an outcome on the affected State on matters in which it has free choice.",Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,
Canada,CAN,4.7 Is intent a constituent element of coercion?,Ambiguous,"¶22....activities would cause coercive effects that deprive, compel, or impose an outcome on the affected State on matters in which it has free choice. ¶23. In its most serious form, coercion may arise through the threat or use of force but could also arise where a cyber activity is designed to deprive the affected State of its freedom of choice. Coercion must be distinguished from other conduct such as public diplomacy, criticism, persuasion, and propaganda.",Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,
Canada,CAN,"4.8 Must the action(s) constituting coercion be designed/intended to influence outcomes in, or conduct with respect to, a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?",Ambiguous,"¶23. In its most serious form, coercion may arise through the threat or use of force but could also arise where a cyber activity is designed to deprive the affected State of its freedom of choice. Coercion must be distinguished from other conduct such as public diplomacy, criticism, persuasion, and propaganda.",Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,
