Canada,CAN,7.2 Does the prohibition proscribe intervention in a state’s external (foreign) affairs?,Yes,"¶22. State cyber activities may breach the foundational international law prohibition of intervention in the internal or external affairs of another State. This would be the case where both of the following conditions are met: the activities aim to interfere with the internal or external affairs of the affected State involving its inherently sovereign functions, known as domaine réservé; and...",Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,
Canada,CAN,7.3 Is the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives referred to as domaine réservé?,Yes,"¶22....the activities aim to interfere with the internal or external affairs of the affected State involving its inherently sovereign functions, known as domaine réservé; and...",Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,
Canada,CAN,"7.4 Is the domaine réservé limited to matters involving the state’s choice of a political, economic, social, and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy?",Silent,,,,
Canada,CAN,7.5 Does international law regulation over a matter of sovereign prerogative remove it from the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Canada,CAN,7.6 Is critical infrastructure part of the domaine réservé?,Yes,"¶24. An example of a prohibited intervention would be a malicious cyber activity that hacks and disables a State’s election commission days before an election, preventing a significant number of citizens from voting, and ultimately influencing the election outcome. Another example would be a malicious cyber activity that disrupts the functioning of a major gas pipeline, compelling the affected State to change its position in bilateral negotiations surrounding an international energy accord.",Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,
Canada,CAN,7.7 Are elections part of the domaine réservé?,Yes,"¶24. An example of a prohibited intervention would be a malicious cyber activity that hacks and disables a State’s election commission days before an election, preventing a significant number of citizens from voting, and ultimately influencing the election outcome.",Canada Statement on International Law Applicable in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/HNH3-BX5J,
