China,CHN,1. Is the prohibition of non-intervention a norm of customary international law?,Ambiguous,"¶1. The UN Charter and the principles enshrined in it, including sovereign equality, refraining from the use or threat of force, settlement of international disputes by peaceful means and non-intervention in the internal affairs of other States, apply in cyberspace. The application of these principles is the cornerstone of the peace, security and stability in cyberspace.","China’s Positions on International Rules-making in Cyberspace, pg. 2, § III, ¶1 (2021).",https://perma.cc/G2JV-C5FF,
China,CHN,2. Is the scope of the principle of non-intervention uncertain?,Silent,,,,
China,CHN,3. Does the prohibition of non-intervention apply to cyber-operations?,Yes,"¶1. The UN Charter and the principles enshrined in it, including sovereign equality, refraining from the use or threat of force, settlement of international disputes by peaceful means and non-intervention in the internal affairs of other States, apply in cyberspace. The application of these principles is the cornerstone of the peace, security and stability in cyberspace.  §II. No State shall intervene in other States’ rights to survival, security and development in cyberspace. No State shall support or allow separatist forces to undermine other States' territorial integrity, national security and social stability through use of ICTs","China’s Positions on International Rules-making in Cyberspace, pg. 2, § III, ¶1 (2021). China’s Views on the Application of the Principle of Sovereignty in Cyberspace",https://perma.cc/MUU6-5NYZ,
