Colombia,COL,1. Is the prohibition of non-intervention a norm of customary international law?,Yes,"¶1. The obligation of non-intervention, a principle of Customary International Law, prohibits States from coercively interfering in the inherent functions of the State, such as their political, economic, or social systems and foreign policy.","Colombia’s National Position on International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 9, §4, ¶1 (2025).",https://perma.cc/9R5K-K447,
Colombia,COL,2. Is the scope of the principle of non-intervention uncertain?,Silent,,,,
Colombia,COL,3. Does the prohibition of non-intervention apply to cyber-operations?,Yes,"¶2.  An act, including a cyber operation, that affects matters within a State’s ""domaine reserve” and is coercive in nature may violate the principle of non-intervention.","Colombia’s National Position on International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 9, §4, ¶2 (2025).",https://perma.cc/9R5K-K447,
Colombia,COL,4. Is coercion a constituent element of prohibited intervention?,Yes,"¶2.  An act, including a cyber operation, that affects matters within a State’s ""domaine reserve” and is coercive in nature may violate the principle of non-intervention.","Colombia’s National Position on International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 9, §4, ¶2 (2025).",https://perma.cc/9R5K-K447,
Colombia,COL,4.1 Is coercion limited to the employment of physical force?,No,"[10 ] Coercion can be understood in two ways: either as an act designed to compel a State to change its behavior or as an act that deprives a State of its control over these matters. While military interventions are clear cases of coercion, non-forcible interference, such as economic or political coercion, is more complex and context-dependent, with cyber operations often cited as an example.",Colombia’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/9R5K-K447,
Colombia,COL,4.2 Can dictatorial demands or acts of compulsion or imposition constitute coercion?,Yes,"[10 ] Coercion can be understood in two ways: either as an act designed to compel a State to change its behavior or as an act that deprives a State of its control over these matters. While military interventions are clear cases of coercion, non-forcible interference, such as economic or political coercion, is more complex and context-dependent, with cyber operations often cited as an example.",Colombia’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/9R5K-K447,
Colombia,COL,4.3 Does the deprivation of a State’s ability to control matters within its domaine réservé amount to coercion?,Yes,"¶2.  An act, including a cyber operation, that affects matters within a State’s ""domaine reserve” and is coercive in nature may violate the principle of non-intervention. Fn. 10. Coercion can be understood in two ways: either as an act designed to compel a State to change its behavior or as an act that deprives a State of its control over these matters. While military interventions are clear cases of coercion, non-forcible interference, such as economic or political coercion, is more complex and context-dependent, with cyber operations often cited as an example.","Colombia’s National Position on International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 9, §4, ¶2; pg. 18, fn. 10 (2025).",https://perma.cc/9R5K-K447,
Colombia,COL,4.4 Does coercion extend to any affirmative acts designed to deprive another state of its freedom of choice with regard to a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?,Ambiguous,"[10 ] Coercion can be understood in two ways: either as an act designed to compel a State to change its behavior or as an act that deprives a State of its control over these matters. While military interventions are clear cases of coercion, non-forcible interference, such as economic or political coercion, is more complex and context-dependent, with cyber operations often cited as an example.",Colombia’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/9R5K-K447,
Colombia,COL,4.5 Must there be a causal nexus between the responsible state’s actions/efforts and the effects on the target state’s internal or external affairs?,Silent,,,,
Colombia,COL,4.6 Can attempts to coerce an outcome amount to prohibited intervention?,Silent,,,,
Colombia,COL,4.7 Is intent a constituent element of coercion?,Ambiguous,"[10 ] Coercion can be understood in two ways: either as an act designed to compel a State to change its behavior or as an act that deprives a State of its control over these matters. While military interventions are clear cases of coercion, non-forcible interference, such as economic or political coercion, is more complex and context-dependent, with cyber operations often cited as an example.",Colombia’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/9R5K-K447,
Colombia,COL,"4.8 Must the action(s) constituting coercion be designed/intended to influence outcomes in, or conduct with respect to, a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?",Ambiguous,"[10 ] Coercion can be understood in two ways: either as an act designed to compel a State to change its behavior or as an act that deprives a State of its control over these matters. While military interventions are clear cases of coercion, non-forcible interference, such as economic or political coercion, is more complex and context-dependent, with cyber operations often cited as an example.",Colombia’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/9R5K-K447,
Colombia,COL,4.9  Can coercion be indirect?,Yes,"¶3.  In accordance with the principle of non-intervention, States should not intervene directly or indirectly in the internal or external affairs of another State, including through ICTs.",Colombia’s National Position on International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/9R5K-K447,
Colombia,COL,4.10 Must the target state have knowledge of the coercive cyber operation constituting the purported intervention?,Silent,,,,
Colombia,COL,5. Does intervention include forms of persuasion or propaganda?,Silent,,,,
Colombia,COL,6. Is there a de minimis exception to the prohibition?,Silent,,,,
Colombia,COL,"7. Must the intervention be directed at matters over which the victim state is permitted by the principle of sovereignty, to decide freely?",Yes,"¶1. The obligation of non-intervention, a principle of Customary International Law, prohibits States from coercively interfering in the inherent functions of the State, such as their political, economic, or social systems and foreign policy.",Colombia’s National Position on International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/9R5K-K447,
Colombia,COL,7.1 Does the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives include only internal affairs?,No,"¶1. The obligation of non-intervention, a principle of Customary International Law, prohibits States from coercively interfering in the inherent functions of the State, such as their political, economic, or social systems and foreign policy. [8] Internal or external affairs of other States.",Colombia’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/9R5K-K447,
Colombia,COL,7.2 Does the prohibition proscribe intervention in a state’s external (foreign) affairs?,Yes,"¶1. The obligation of non-intervention, a principle of Customary International Law, prohibits States from coercively interfering in the inherent functions of the State, such as their political, economic, or social systems and foreign policy. ¶3. In accordance with the principle of non-intervention, States should not intervene directly or indirectly in the internal or external affairs of another State, including through ICTs.",Colombia’s National Position on International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/9R5K-K447,
Colombia,COL,7.3 Is the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives referred to as domaine réservé?,Yes,"¶2. An act, including a cyber operation, that affects matters within a State’s ""domaine réservé"" [9] and is coercive in nature may violate the principle of non-intervention. [9] Areas where the State is free to decide, for example, like national elections or foreign policy,","Colombia’s National Position on International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 9, §4, ¶2;",https://perma.cc/9R5K-K447,
Colombia,COL,"7.4 Is the domaine réservé limited to matters involving the state’s choice of a political, economic, social, and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy?",Ambiguous,"¶1. The obligation of non-intervention, a principle of Customary International Law, prohibits States from coercively interfering in the inherent functions of the State, such as their political, economic, or social systems and foreign policy. ¶2. An act, including a cyber operation, that affects matters within a State’s ""domaine réservé""[9] and is coercive in nature may violate the principle of non-intervention.",Colombia’s National Position on International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/9R5K-K447,
Colombia,COL,7.5 Does international law regulation over a matter of sovereign prerogative remove it from the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Colombia,COL,7.6 Is critical infrastructure part of the domaine réservé?,Ambiguous,"¶3. In accordance with the principle of non-intervention, States should not intervene directly or indirectly in the internal or external affairs of another State, including through ICTs. Such would be the case, for instance, of interference with the electoral systems of another State, or cyber activities directed at interfering with the operation of the systems of public agencies or institutions in another state.",Colombia’s National Position on International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/9R5K-K447,
Colombia,COL,7.7 Are elections part of the domaine réservé?,Yes,"¶3. In accordance with the principle of non-intervention, States should not intervene directly or indirectly in the internal or external affairs of another State, including through ICTs. Such would be the case, for instance, of interference with the electoral systems of another State, or cyber activities directed at interfering with the operation of the systems of public agencies or institutions in another state.",Colombia’s National Position on International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/9R5K-K447,
Colombia,COL,7.8 Is the operation of legislatures a part of the domaine réservé?,Ambiguous,"¶3. In accordance with the principle of non-intervention, States should not intervene directly or indirectly in the internal or external affairs of another State, including through ICTs. Such would be the case, for instance, of interference with the electoral systems of another State, or cyber activities directed at interfering with the operation of the systems of public agencies or institutions in another state.",Colombia’s National Position on International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/9R5K-K447,
Colombia,COL,7.9 Is financial system stability part of the domaine réservé?,Ambiguous,See question 7.6,,,
Colombia,COL,7.10 Are medical services part of the domaine réservé?,Ambiguous,See question 7.6,,,
Costa Rica,CRI,1. Is the prohibition of non-intervention a norm of customary international law?,Yes,¶23. The principle of non-intervention is grounded in customary international law and prohibits States from interfering directly or indirectly with matters within the domestic jurisdiction of other States...,Costa Rica’s Position on The Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,
Costa Rica,CRI,2. Is the scope of the principle of non-intervention uncertain?,Silent,,,,
Costa Rica,CRI,3. Does the prohibition of non-intervention apply to cyber-operations?,Yes,"¶25. In Costa Rica’s view, these various forms of coercion may well be carried out in or through ICTs and amount to violations of the principle of non-intervention insofar as they interfere with a State’s internal or external affairs.",Costa Rica’s Position on The Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,
Costa Rica,CRI,4. Is coercion a constituent element of prohibited intervention?,Yes,"¶23. …Moreover, according to the ICJ, a wrongful intervention is one which ‘uses methods of coercion in regard to such choices, which must remain free ones’  ¶25. In Costa Rica’s view, these various forms of coercion may well be carried out in or through ICTs and amount to violations of the principle of non-intervention insofar as they interfere with a State’s internal or external affairs.",Costa Rica’s Position on The Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,
Costa Rica,CRI,4.1 Is coercion limited to the employment of physical force?,No,"¶24. Coercion is clear-cut when a State uses or threatens to use force against another one. Nonetheless, it can also occur in a multitude of ways where one State, directly or indirectly through support for non-State actors, deprives another State of the capacity to make free and informed choices pertaining to its internal or external affairs.",Costa Rica’s Position on The Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,
Costa Rica,CRI,4.2 Can dictatorial demands or acts of compulsion or imposition constitute coercion?,Ambiguous,"¶24. Mere threats of intervention or acts seeking to interfere within another State’s domaine reservé may also breach the principle. For such breaches to occur, it suffices that a State intends to coerce another State, employs coercive methods, or eventually causes coercive effects in another State.",Costa Rica’s Position on The Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,
Costa Rica,CRI,4.3 Does the deprivation of a State’s ability to control matters within its domaine réservé amount to coercion?,Silent,,,,
Costa Rica,CRI,4.4 Does coercion extend to any affirmative acts designed to deprive another state of its freedom of choice with regard to a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?,Yes,"¶24. Coercion is clear-cut when a State uses or threatens to use force against another one. Nonetheless, it can also occur in a multitude of ways where one State, directly or indirectly through support for non-State actors, deprives another State of the capacity to make free and informed choices pertaining to its internal or external affairs. Coercion may occur when a State provides financial or other forms of support to secessionist, subversive or violent groups in the territory of another State, when it exercises significant political or economic pressure on another State, or when it engages in or supports subversive or hostile propaganda or the dissemination of false news that interfere in the internal or external affairs of another State …",Costa Rica’s Position on The Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,
Costa Rica,CRI,4.5 Must there be a causal nexus between the responsible state’s actions/efforts and the effects on the target state’s internal or external affairs?,Silent,,,,
Costa Rica,CRI,4.6 Can attempts to coerce an outcome amount to prohibited intervention?,Yes,"¶24. Moreover, coercion needs not be successful in intervening within a State’s internal or external affairs. Mere threats of intervention or acts seeking to interfere within another State’s domaine reservé may also breach the principle",Costa Rica’s Position on The Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,
Costa Rica,CRI,4.7 Is intent a constituent element of coercion?,Ambiguous,"¶24. Moreover, coercion needs not be successful in intervening within a State’s internal or external affairs. Mere threats of intervention or acts seeking to interfere within another State’s domaine reservé may also breach the principle. For such breaches to occur, it suffices that a State intends to coerce another State, employs coercive methods, or eventually causes coercive effects in another State",Costa Rica’s Position on The Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,
Costa Rica,CRI,"4.8 Must the action(s) constituting coercion be designed/intended to influence outcomes in, or conduct with respect to, a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?",Yes,"¶24. Coercion is clear-cut when a State uses or threatens to use force against another one. Nonetheless, it can also occur in a multitude of ways where one State, directly or indirectly through support for non-State actors, deprives another State of the capacity to make free and informed choices pertaining to its internal or external affairs. Coercion may occur when a State provides financial or other forms of support to secessionist, subversive or violent groups in the territory of another State, when it exercises significant political or economic pressure on another State, or when it engages in or supports subversive or hostile propaganda or the dissemination of false news that interfere in the internal or external affairs of another State. Moreover, coercion needs not be successful in intervening within a State’s internal or external affairs. Mere threats of intervention or acts seeking to interfere within another State’s domaine reservé may also breach the principle. For such breaches to occur, it suffices that a State intends to coerce another State, employs coercive methods, or eventually causes coercive effects in another State.",Costa Rica’s Position on The Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,
Costa Rica,CRI,4.9  Can coercion be indirect?,Yes,"¶24. Coercion is clear-cut when a State uses or threatens to use force against another one. Nonetheless, it can also occur in a multitude of ways where one State, directly or indirectly through support for non-State actors, deprives another State of the capacity to make free and informed choices pertaining to its internal or external affairs.",Costa Rica’s Position on The Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,
Costa Rica,CRI,4.10 Must the target state have knowledge of the coercive cyber operation constituting the purported intervention?,Silent,,,,
Costa Rica,CRI,5. Does intervention include forms of persuasion or propaganda?,Yes,"¶24. Coercion may occur when a State provides financial or other forms of support to secessionist, subversive or violent groups in the territory of another State, when it exercises significant political or economic pressure on another State, or when it engages in or supports subversive or hostile propaganda or the dissemination of false news that interfere in the internal or external affairs of another State.",Costa Rica’s Position on The Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,
Costa Rica,CRI,6. Is there a de minimis exception to the prohibition?,Silent,,,,
Costa Rica,CRI,"7. Must the intervention be directed at matters over which the victim state is permitted by the principle of sovereignty, to decide freely?",Yes,"¶23. According to the ICJ, a prohibited intervention is one bearing ‘on matters in which each State is permitted, by the principle of State sovereignty, to decide freely’. Examples include ‘the choice of a political, economic, social and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy’, whether these are carried out by private or public entities, and irrespective of a State’s new undertakings under international law. Moreover, according to the ICJ, a wrongful intervention is one which ‘uses methods of coercion in regard to such choices, which must remain free ones’",Costa Rica’s Position on The Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,
Costa Rica,CRI,7.1 Does the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives include only internal affairs?,No,"¶25. In Costa Rica’s view, these various forms of coercion may well be carried out in or through ICTs and amount to violations of the principle of non-intervention insofar as they interfere with a State’s internal or external affairs.",Costa Rica’s Position on The Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,
Costa Rica,CRI,7.2 Does the prohibition proscribe intervention in a state’s external (foreign) affairs?,Yes,"¶25. In Costa Rica’s view, these various forms of coercion may well be carried out in or through ICTs and amount to violations of the principle of non-intervention insofar as they interfere with a State’s internal or external affairs.",Costa Rica’s Position on The Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,
Costa Rica,CRI,7.3 Is the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives referred to as domaine réservé?,Yes,¶24. Mere threats of intervention or acts seeking to interfere within another State’s domaine reservé may also breach the principle.,Costa Rica’s Position on The Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,
Costa Rica,CRI,"7.4 Is the domaine réservé limited to matters involving the state’s choice of a political, economic, social, and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy?",Ambiguous,"¶23. According to the ICJ, a prohibited intervention is one bearing ‘on matters in which each State is permitted, by the principle of State sovereignty, to decide freely’. Examples include ‘the choice of a political, economic, social and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy’... ¶25. In Costa Rica’s view, these various forms of coercion may well be carried out in or through ICTs and amount to violations of the principle of non-intervention insofar as they interfere with a State’s internal or external affairs…",Costa Rica’s Position on The Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,
Costa Rica,CRI,7.5 Does international law regulation over a matter of sovereign prerogative remove it from the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Costa Rica,CRI,7.6 Is critical infrastructure part of the domaine réservé?,Yes,"¶25. A prominent example of a breach of non-intervention are ransomware attacks crippling or simply interfering with a State’s ability to run public services, such as finance, education, and social security.",Costa Rica’s Position on The Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,
Costa Rica,CRI,7.7 Are elections part of the domaine réservé?,Yes,"¶25. Moreover, foreign election interference may also infringe the principle of non-intervention. This may take the form of cyber operations directly interfering with mail ballots or voter databases, or electoral disinformation campaigns seeking to mislead the electorate about the vote itself, candidates, electoral polls or results.",Costa Rica’s Position on The Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,
Costa Rica,CRI,7.8 Is the operation of legislatures a part of the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Costa Rica,CRI,7.9 Is financial system stability part of the domaine réservé?,Yes,"¶25. A prominent example of a breach of non-intervention are ransomware attacks crippling or simply interfering with a State’s ability to run public services, such as finance, education, and social security.",Costa Rica’s Position on The Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,
Costa Rica,CRI,7.10 Are medical services part of the domaine réservé?,Yes,"¶25. Other types of disinformation, such as those affecting a State’s health policies, may also amount to a prohibited intervention.",Costa Rica’s Position on The Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/7483-K9DH,
Cuba,CUB,1. Is the prohibition of non-intervention a norm of customary international law?,Ambiguous,"¶24. The above-mentioned challenges cannot be met with the fragmented and diverse regulations of national jurisdictions and with supposed voluntary standards of good conduct that are easily ignored, without any legal consequences. ¶25. In this context, the implementation of the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations in cyberspace, particularly the principles of sovereign equality, territorial integrity, non-intervention in the internal affairs of States, refraining from the use or threat of use of force against the territorial integrity and political independence of States, international cooperation for mutual and equitable benefit and interest, and the peaceful settlement of disputes, acquires particular relevance.",Position Paper of the Republic of Cuba on the Application of International Law to Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace,https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:w:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Cuba/Spanish%20to%20English%20Translation%20of%20Cuba%20National%20Position%20(2024).docx?d=wcf77ee55ec104a8a88b3ff719d0ec527&csf=1&web=1&e=1DNfcI,
Cuba,CUB,2. Is the scope of the principle of non-intervention uncertain?,Silent,,,,
Cuba,CUB,3. Does the prohibition of non-intervention apply to cyber-operations?,Yes,"¶2. ... Ultimately, the violation of the obligation not to interfere in the internal affairs of other States is established irrespective of whether Information and Communication Technologies (ICTs) or otherwise were used.",Position Paper of the Republic of Cuba on the Application of International Law to Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace,https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:w:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Cuba/Spanish%20to%20English%20Translation%20of%20Cuba%20National%20Position%20(2024).docx?d=wcf77ee55ec104a8a88b3ff719d0ec527&csf=1&web=1&e=1DNfcI,
Cuba,CUB,4. Is coercion a constituent element of prohibited intervention?,Silent,,,,
Cuba,CUB,4.1 Is coercion limited to the employment of physical force?,Ambiguous,"¶6. The concept of the use of force has been analyzed from a perspective that involves the notions of armed violence, coercive force or force of interference. From this point of view, it is understood that only armed physical force is prohibited by Article 2(4) of the Unite Nations Charter and that the definition of armed attack is much more restrictive than that of use of force. Under current international law, a cyber action does not constitute an armed attack in accordance with Article 2(4), since it lacks the physical characteristics and other requirements that define the aforementioned military attacks, their effects and legal consequences. ¶8. Considering the misuse of ICTs as an act of aggression or war should inevitably lead to giving the same qualification to other contemporary practices, such as the application of sanctions or unilateral coercive measures of financial, economic, and political pressure. In contrast to the possible and often hypothetical scenarios of damage resulting from the misuse of ICTs, international evidence does point to the existence of devastating effects of the application of unilateral coercive measures.""",Position Paper of the Republic of Cuba on the Application of International Law to Information and Communication Technologies in Cyberspace,https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:w:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/Cuba/Spanish%20to%20English%20Translation%20of%20Cuba%20National%20Position%20(2024).docx?d=wcf77ee55ec104a8a88b3ff719d0ec527&csf=1&web=1&e=1DNfcI,
