Czech Republic,CZE,1. Is the prohibition of non-intervention a norm of customary international law?,Yes,"¶7. The obligation of non-intervention is a well-established customary rule of international law stemming from the principle of State sovereignty, which is also applicable to cyberspace.  First, as stated by the GGE and many of my colleagues here, existing international law applies to cyberspace in its entirety. Indeed, existing international law provides us with all the necessary tools to prevent actual conflicts in cyber domain. The issue at stake is not a gap in existing law, but compliance with existing law and reaching a common understanding on how to apply the law to today’s environment.",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace Statement of the Special Envoy for Cyberspace and Director of Cybersecurity Department of the Czech Republic,https://perma.cc/68JC-6HQ6,
Czech Republic,CZE,2. Is the scope of the principle of non-intervention uncertain?,Silent,,,,
Czech Republic,CZE,3. Does the prohibition of non-intervention apply to cyber-operations?,Yes,"¶7. The obligation of non-intervention is a well-established customary rule of international law stemming from the principle of State sovereignty, which is also applicable to cyberspace.",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,
Czech Republic,CZE,4. Is coercion a constituent element of prohibited intervention?,Yes,"¶9. There are two conditions for a cyber activity to be considered as an intervention prohibited under international law:  ... b) The activity must be coercive in nature. Coercion is an activity intended to deprive, either directly or indirectly, the State of its ability to exercise control or govern matters within its internal and external affairs. The precise definition of coercion, and thus of unauthorised intervention, has not yet fully crystallised in customary international law, further State practice and opinio juris are therefore needed.",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,
Czech Republic,CZE,4.1 Is coercion limited to the employment of physical force?,Ambiguous,"¶9b) The activity must be coercive in nature. Coercion is an activity intended to deprive, either directly or indirectly, the State of its ability to exercise control or govern matters within its internal and external affairs. The precise definition of coercion, and thus of unauthorised intervention, has not yet fully crystallised in customary international law, further State practice and opinio juris are therefore needed.",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,
Czech Republic,CZE,4.2 Can dictatorial demands or acts of compulsion or imposition constitute coercion?,Silent,,,,
Czech Republic,CZE,4.3 Does the deprivation of a State’s ability to control matters within its domaine réservé amount to coercion?,Yes,"¶9. There are two conditions for a cyber activity to be considered as an intervention prohibited under international law:  a) The activity has to tamper with the internal or external affairs of the State, also known as domaine réservé. Internal affairs include, for instance, the conduct of elections; court proceedings; or the drafting and enactment of laws. External affairs include, for instance, the ratification and denunciation of treaties; consenting to official activities of other State’s organs in its territory; or opening or closing down an embassy.  b) The activity must be coercive in nature. Coercion is an activity intended to deprive, either directly or indirectly, the State of its ability to exercise control or govern matters within its internal and external affairs. The precise definition of coercion, and thus of unauthorised intervention, has not yet fully crystallised in customary international law, further State practice and opinio juris are therefore needed.",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,
Czech Republic,CZE,4.4 Does coercion extend to any affirmative acts designed to deprive another state of its freedom of choice with regard to a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?,Ambiguous,"¶9. There are two conditions for a cyber activity to be considered as an intervention prohibited under international law:  ... b) The activity must be coercive in nature. Coercion is an activity intended to deprive, either directly or indirectly, the State of its ability to exercise control or govern matters within its internal and external affairs. The precise definition of coercion, and thus of unauthorised intervention, has not yet fully crystallised in customary international law, further State practice and opinio juris are therefore needed.",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,
Czech Republic,CZE,4.5 Must there be a causal nexus between the responsible state’s actions/efforts and the effects on the target state’s internal or external affairs?,Yes,"¶10. Furthermore, a causal nexus between the activity and the effect on the target State to change its behaviour has to be considered. The assessment whether a particular cyber activity has reached the threshold of prohibited intervention has to be done on a case-by-case basis.",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,
Czech Republic,CZE,4.6 Can attempts to coerce an outcome amount to prohibited intervention?,Ambiguous,"¶9. There are two conditions for a cyber activity to be considered as an intervention prohibited under international law: a) The activity has to tamper with the internal or external affairs of the State, also known as domaine réservé. Internal affairs include, for instance, the conduct of elections; court proceedings; or the drafting and enactment of laws. External affairs include, for instance, the ratification and denunciation of treaties; consenting to official activities of other State’s organs in its territory; or opening or closing down an embassy. b) The activity must be coercive in nature. Coercion is an activity intended to deprive, either directly or indirectly, the State of its ability to exercise control or govern matters within its internal and external affairs. The precise definition of coercion, and thus of unauthorised intervention, has not yet fully crystallised in customary international law, further State practice and opinio juris are therefore needed.",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,
Czech Republic,CZE,4.7 Is intent a constituent element of coercion?,Yes,"¶9. There are two conditions for a cyber activity to be considered as an intervention prohibited under international law:  ... b) The activity must be coercive in nature. Coercion is an activity intended to deprive, either directly or indirectly, the State of its ability to exercise control or govern matters within its internal and external affairs. The precise definition of coercion, and thus of unauthorised intervention, has not yet fully crystallised in customary international law, further State practice and opinio juris are therefore needed.   ¶11. There is a certain similarity between the terms “internal and external affairs” and “inherently governmental affairs”, which is one of the defining elements of violation of sovereignty. The difference between actions that violate sovereignty and actions that violate the prohibition of intervention is that the latter is coercive, i.e., intentionally aims to influence the State’s free will and choice",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,
Czech Republic,CZE,"4.8 Must the action(s) constituting coercion be designed/intended to influence outcomes in, or conduct with respect to, a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?",Yes,"¶11. There is a certain similarity between the terms “internal and external affairs” and “inherently governmental affairs”, which is one of the defining elements of violation of sovereignty. The difference between actions that violate sovereignty and actions that violate the prohibition of intervention is that the latter is coercive, i.e., intentionally aims to influence the State’s free will and choice.",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,
Czech Republic,CZE,4.9  Can coercion be indirect?,Yes,"¶9. There are two conditions for a cyber activity to be considered as an intervention prohibited under international law:  ... b) The activity must be coercive in nature. Coercion is an activity intended to deprive, either directly or indirectly, the State of its ability to exercise control or govern matters within its internal and external affairs. The precise definition of coercion, and thus of unauthorised intervention, has not yet fully crystallised in customary international law, further State practice and opinio juris are therefore needed.",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,
Czech Republic,CZE,4.10 Must the target state have knowledge of the coercive cyber operation constituting the purported intervention?,Silent,,,,
Czech Republic,CZE,5. Does intervention include forms of persuasion or propaganda?,No,"¶13. Prohibition of intervention does not cover cyber activities broadly described as “propaganda”, provided that they do not violate another specific rule of international law, such as direct and public incitement to commit genocide. Mere influencing, criticism or persuasion do not meet the requirements to be qualified as prohibited intervention either.",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,
Czech Republic,CZE,6. Is there a de minimis exception to the prohibition?,Silent,,,,
Czech Republic,CZE,"7. Must the intervention be directed at matters over which the victim state is permitted by the principle of sovereignty, to decide freely?",Ambiguous,"¶7. A definition of prohibited intervention was provided by the International Court of Justice in the 1986 Nicaragua v United States case: “A prohibited intervention must […] be one bearing on matters in which each State is permitted, by the principle of State sovereignty, to decide freely. One of these is the choice of a political, economic, social, and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy. Intervention is wrongful when it uses methods of coercion in regard to such choices, which must remain free ones.”  Fn. 10. Such matters in which a State may decide freely include political, economic, social, and cultural system as well as formulation of foreign policy.",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,
Czech Republic,CZE,7.1 Does the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives include only internal affairs?,No,"¶9. There are two conditions for a cyber activity to be considered as an intervention prohibited under international law:  a) The activity has to tamper with the internal or external affairs of the State, also known as domaine réservé. Internal affairs include, for instance, the conduct of elections; court proceedings; or the drafting and enactment of laws. External affairs include, for instance, the ratification and denunciation of treaties; consenting to official activities of other State’s organs in its territory; or opening or closing down an embassy.",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,
Czech Republic,CZE,7.2 Does the prohibition proscribe intervention in a state’s external (foreign) affairs?,Yes,"¶9. There are two conditions for a cyber activity to be considered as an intervention prohibited under international law:  a) The activity has to tamper with the internal or external affairs of the State, also known as domaine réservé. Internal affairs include, for instance, the conduct of elections; court proceedings; or the drafting and enactment of laws. External affairs include, for instance, the ratification and denunciation of treaties; consenting to official activities of other State’s organs in its territory; or opening or closing down an embassy.",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,
Czech Republic,CZE,7.3 Is the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives referred to as domaine réservé?,Yes,"¶9. There are two conditions for a cyber activity to be considered as an intervention prohibited under international law:  a) The activity has to tamper with the internal or external affairs of the State, also known as domaine réservé. Internal affairs include, for instance, the conduct of elections; court proceedings; or the drafting and enactment of laws. External affairs include, for instance, the ratification and denunciation of treaties; consenting to official activities of other State’s organs in its territory; or opening or closing down an embassy.",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,
Czech Republic,CZE,"7.4 Is the domaine réservé limited to matters involving the state’s choice of a political, economic, social, and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy?",Ambiguous,"¶7. A definition of prohibited intervention was provided by the International Court of Justice in the 1986 Nicaragua v United States case: “A prohibited intervention must […] be one bearing on matters in which each State is permitted, by the principle of State sovereignty, to decide freely. One of these is the choice of a political, economic, social, and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy. Intervention is wrongful when it uses methods of coercion in regard to such choices, which must remain free ones.”  ¶9. There are two conditions for a cyber activity to be considered as an intervention prohibited under international law:  a) The activity has to tamper with the internal or external affairs of the State, also known as domaine réservé. Internal affairs include, for instance, the conduct of elections; court proceedings; or the drafting and enactment of laws. External affairs include, for instance, the ratification and denunciation of treaties; consenting to official activities of other State’s organs in its territory; or opening or closing down an embassy.",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,
Czech Republic,CZE,7.5 Does international law regulation over a matter of sovereign prerogative remove it from the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Czech Republic,CZE,7.6 Is critical infrastructure part of the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Czech Republic,CZE,7.7 Are elections part of the domaine réservé?,Yes,"¶9. There are two conditions for a cyber activity to be considered as an intervention prohibited under international law:  a) The activity has to tamper with the internal or external affairs of the State, also known as domaine réservé. Internal affairs include, for instance, the conduct of elections; court proceedings; or the drafting and enactment of laws.  ¶12. The use of cyber means to target election systems to manipulate election results would, for example, constitute a violation of the obligation of non-intervention.",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,
Czech Republic,CZE,7.8 Is the operation of legislatures a part of the domaine réservé?,Yes,"¶9. There are two conditions for a cyber activity to be considered as an intervention prohibited under international law:  a) The activity has to tamper with the internal or external affairs of the State, also known as domaine réservé. Internal affairs include, for instance, the conduct of elections; court proceedings; or the drafting and enactment of laws.",Czech Republic Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/35XF-55UV,
Czech Republic,CZE,7.9 Is financial system stability part of the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Czech Republic,CZE,7.10 Are medical services part of the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Denmark,DNK,1. Is the prohibition of non-intervention a norm of customary international law?,Yes,"The principle of non-intervention is a fundamental principle of international law. It is a corollary of the principle of sovereignty, and more specifically the aspect that provides for the sovereign equality of States as set forth in article 2(1) of the UN Charter.  Denmark is of the view that the prohibition of intervention is a rule of international law forming part of customary international law. This was established by the ICJ in the Nicaragua v. United States of America case where the Court held that States are prohibited from intervening directly or indirectly in internal or external affairs of other States.","Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://perma.cc/6D5U-ZXJ8,
Denmark,DNK,2. Is the scope of the principle of non-intervention uncertain?,Ambiguous,"The term coercion is not defined in either treaty law or customary international law. Denmark takes the view that an act may be considered of a coercive nature when the act of interference has a potential for compelling the target State to engage in an action that it would otherwise not take. However, a distinction must be drawn between activities that merely involve influencing, as opposed to compelling, the voluntary actions of a target State.  The scope of activities falling within the domaine réservé include but are not limited to “ (…) the choice of a political, economic, social, and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy.” The range of activities covered by the non-intervention rule largely overlaps with the activities reserved to States under the rule of sovereignty.","Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://perma.cc/6D5U-ZXJ8,
Denmark,DNK,3. Does the prohibition of non-intervention apply to cyber-operations?,Yes,"The UN General Assembly has endorsed the conclusions set out in the reports from the Group of Governmental Experts (GGE) and the Open-ended Working Group on Information and Communication Technologies (OEWG) affirming that international law, including the UN Charter, applies in cyberspace. Denmark is an unwavering supporter of this view and continues to support efforts to develop the rules, norms and principles of responsible State behaviour in cyberspace. As a strong proponent of the rules based international order Denmark is fully committed to international law as the fundamental framework for responsible state behaviour in cyberspace.  It is the view of Denmark that the application of international law does not depend on the particular technological means employed. Treaty obligations, customary law, and general principles of law apply across domains, which includes operations conducted in cyberspace (Toolkit, Denmark 2023) Denmark considers that coercion is not limited to means of direct or indirect use of force and that also measures below this threshold may constitute coercion. Cyber activities that do not amount to use of force can therefore also be coercive.","Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://perma.cc/6D5U-ZXJ8,
Denmark,DNK,4. Is coercion a constituent element of prohibited intervention?,Yes,"The term coercion is not defined in either treaty law or customary international law. Denmark takes the view that an act may be considered of a coercive nature when the act of interference has a potential for compelling the target State to engage in an action that it would otherwise not take. However, a distinction must be drawn between activities that merely involve influencing, as opposed to compelling, the voluntary actions of a target State.  In order for an action to qualify as an unlawful intervention it must qualify as an intervention in matters that are the sovereign prerogative of a State, the so-called domaine réservé, and it must involve an element of coercion","Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://perma.cc/6D5U-ZXJ8,
Denmark,DNK,4.1 Is coercion limited to the employment of physical force?,No,The term coercion is not defined in either treaty law or customary international law. Denmark takes the view that an act may be considered of a coercive nature when the act of interference has a potential for compelling the target State to engage in an action that it would otherwise not take... To be coercive the effort to intervene must be designed to have a decisive impact on outcomes or conduct with respect to a matter reserved to the target State. As emphasized by the Court in the Nicaragua judgment coercive acts involving the use of force are particularly obvious examples of unlawful interventions.  Denmark considers that coercion is not limited to means of direct or indirect use of force and that also measures below this threshold may constitute coercion. Cyber activities that do not amount to use of force can therefore also be coercive.,"Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://perma.cc/6D5U-ZXJ8,
Denmark,DNK,4.2 Can dictatorial demands or acts of compulsion or imposition constitute coercion?,Ambiguous,Denmark takes the view that an act may be considered of a coercive nature when the act of interference has a potential for compelling the target State to engage in an action that it would otherwise not take,"Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://perma.cc/6D5U-ZXJ8,
Denmark,DNK,4.3 Does the deprivation of a State’s ability to control matters within its domaine réservé amount to coercion?,Silent,,,,
Denmark,DNK,4.4 Does coercion extend to any affirmative acts designed to deprive another state of its freedom of choice with regard to a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?,Yes,Denmark takes the view that an act may be considered of a coercive nature when the act of interference has a potential for compelling the target State to engage in an action that it would otherwise not take … To be coercive the effort to intervene must be designed to have a decisive impact on outcomes or conduct with respect to a matter reserved to the target State.,"Denmark’s Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,",https://perma.cc/6D5U-ZXJ8,
