European Union,EUN,1. Is the prohibition of non-intervention a norm of customary international law?,Yes,§2. The principle of non-intervention is a well-established rule of customary international law.,Declaration on a Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/EQX2-GMNU,
European Union,EUN,Is the scope of the principle of non-intervention uncertain?,Silent,,,,
European Union,EUN,3. Does the prohibition of non-intervention apply to cyber-operations?,Yes,"§2. ...coercive interference with ICT systems, cloud-services and networks on another State’s territory or within its jurisdiction without its consent, within its domaine réservé, can constitute a prohibited intervention under international law.",Declaration on a Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/EQX2-GMNU,
European Union,EUN,4. Is coercion a constituent element of prohibited intervention?,Yes,§2. An activity that is attributable to a State qualifies as a prohibited intervention if it coercively interferes with matters in which each State may decide freely.,Declaration on a Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/EQX2-GMNU,
European Union,EUN,4.1 Is coercion limited to the employment of physical force?,No,"§2. Coercion could mean to compel another State to involuntarily pursue or forego a course of action. Accordingly, coercive interference with ICT systems, cloud-services and networks on another State’s territory or within its jurisdiction without its consent, within its domaine réservé, can constitute a prohibited intervention.",Declaration on a Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/EQX2-GMNU,
European Union,EUN,4.2 Can dictatorial demands or acts of compulsion or imposition constitute coercion?,Yes,§2. Coercion could mean to compel another State to involuntarily pursue or forego a course of action.,Declaration on a Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/EQX2-GMNU,
European Union,EUN,4.3 Does the deprivation of a State’s ability to control matters within its domaine réservé amount to coercion?,Silent,,,,
European Union,EUN,4.4 Does coercion extend to any affirmative acts designed to deprive another state of its freedom of choice with regard to a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?,Silent,,,,
European Union,EUN,4.5 Must there be a causal nexus between the responsible state’s actions/efforts and the effects on the target state’s internal or external affairs?,Silent,,,,
European Union,EUN,4.6 Can attempts to coerce an outcome amount to prohibited intervention?,Silent,,,,
European Union,EUN,4.7 Is intent a constituent element of coercion?,Silent,,,,
European Union,EUN,"4.8 Must the action(s) constituting coercion be designed/intended to influence outcomes in, or conduct with respect to, a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?",Silent,,,,
European Union,EUN,4.9   Can coercion be indirect?,Yes,§2. The principle of non-intervention is a well-established rule of customary international law and is a corollary of sovereignty and the sovereign equality of all States. It prohibits a State from directly or indirectly intervening in the affairs of another State.,Declaration on a Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/EQX2-GMNU,
European Union,EUN,4.10 Must the target state have knowledge of the coercive cyber operation constituting the purported intervention?,Silent,,,,
European Union,EUN,5. Does intervention include forms of persuasion or propaganda?,Silent,,,,
European Union,EUN,6. Is there a de minimis exception to the prohibition?,Silent,,,,
European Union,EUN,"7. Must the intervention be directed at matters over which the victim state is permitted by the principle of sovereignty, to decide freely?",Yes,§2. An activity that is attributable to a State qualifies as a prohibited intervention if it coercively interferes with matters in which each State may decide freely as stated by the International Court of Justice in the Nicaragua Judgement.,Declaration on a Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/EQX2-GMNU,
European Union,EUN,7.1 Does the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives include only internal affairs?,Ambiguous,§2. The principle of non-intervention is a well-established rule of customary international law and is a corollary of sovereignty and the sovereign equality of all States. It prohibits a State from directly or indirectly intervening in the affairs of another State. An activity that is attributable to a State qualifies as a prohibited intervention if it coercively interferes with matters in which each State may decide freely.,Declaration on a Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/EQX2-GMNU,
European Union,EUN,7.2 Does the prohibition proscribe intervention in a state’s external (foreign) affairs?,Ambiguous,§2....prohibits a State from directly or indirectly intervening in the affairs of another State.,Declaration on a Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/EQX2-GMNU,
European Union,EUN,7.3 Is the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives referred to as domaine réservé?,Yes,"§2. coercive interference with ICT systems, cloud-services and networks on another State’s territory or within its jurisdiction without its consent, within its domaine réservé, can constitute a prohibited intervention under international law if it is attributable to a State.",Declaration on a Common Understanding of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/EQX2-GMNU,
European Union,EUN,"7.4 Is the domaine réservé limited to matters involving the state’s choice of a political, economic, social, and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy?",Silent,,,,
