Finland,FIN,1. Is the prohibition of non-intervention a norm of customary international law?,Yes,A hostile interference by cyber means may also breach the customary prohibition of intervention in the internal affairs of another State.,International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://perma.cc/M5DA-YSE6,
Finland,FIN,2. Is the scope of the principle of non-intervention uncertain?,Yes,"The principle of State sovereignty has also given rise to a number of specific rules, such as those prohibiting the use of force and intervention in the internal affairs of other States. Below the threshold of prohibited intervention, all States have an obligation to refrain from acts that violate the territorial integrity or political independence of other States. A hostile interference by cyber means may also breach the customary prohibition of intervention in the internal affairs of another State... Most open questions relate to the element of coercion and to how it manifests itself in cyber operations.",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://perma.cc/M5DA-YSE6,
Finland,FIN,3. Does the prohibition of non-intervention apply to cyber-operations?,Yes,"A hostile interference by cyber means may also breach the customary prohibition of intervention in the internal affairs of another State, provided that it is done with the purpose of compelling or coercing that State in relation to affairs regarding which it has free choice (so-called domaine réservé).",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://perma.cc/M5DA-YSE6,
Finland,FIN,4. Is coercion a constituent element of prohibited intervention?,Yes,... provided that it is done with the purpose of compelling or coercing that State in relation to affairs regarding which it has free choice (so-called domaine réservé). The requirement of coercion leaves out lesser forms of influence and persuasion that are commonplace in international relations,International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://perma.cc/M5DA-YSE6,
Finland,FIN,4.1 Is coercion limited to the employment of physical force?,No,"Hacking of voter databases or manipulation of vote counts in order to alter the election results has nevertheless been recognized as a fairly clear case.  To be comparable to a real world intervention, cyber interference must also be of a serious nature.   Military or economic pressure may also qualify as coercion. Hostile cyber interference done with the purpose of promoting or supporting armed action in another State could constitute an example of prohibited intervention, provided that it seeks to force a certain policy change.",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://perma.cc/M5DA-YSE6,
Finland,FIN,4.2 Can dictatorial demands or acts of compulsion or imposition constitute coercion?,Silent,,,,
Finland,FIN,4.3 Does the deprivation of a State’s ability to control matters within its domaine réservé amount to coercion?,Silent,,,,
Finland,FIN,4.4 Does coercion extend to any affirmative acts designed to deprive another state of its freedom of choice with regard to a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?,Yes,"A hostile interference by cyber means may also breach the customary prohibition of intervention in the internal affairs of another State, provided that it is done with the purpose of compelling or coercing that State in relation to affairs regarding which it has free choice (so-called domaine réservé).",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://perma.cc/M5DA-YSE6,
Finland,FIN,4.5 Must there be a causal nexus between the responsible state’s actions/efforts and the effects on the target state’s internal or external affairs?,Silent,,,,
Finland,FIN,4.6 Can attempts to coerce an outcome amount to prohibited intervention?,Silent,,,,
Finland,FIN,4.7 Is intent a constituent element of coercion?,Yes,"A hostile interference by cyber means may also breach the customary prohibition of intervention in the internal affairs of another State, provided that it is done with the purpose of compelling or coercing that State in relation to affairs regarding which it has free choice (so-called domaine réservé).",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://perma.cc/M5DA-YSE6,
Finland,FIN,"4.8 Must the action(s) constituting coercion be designed/intended to influence outcomes in, or conduct with respect to, a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?",Yes,"A hostile interference by cyber means may also breach the customary prohibition of intervention in the internal affairs of another State, provided that it is done with the purpose of compelling or coercing that State in relation to affairs regarding which it has free choice (so-called domaine réservé).    Compared to a violation of sovereignty, the requirement of coercive nature and that of domaine réservé make the threshold of prohibited intervention considerably higher.",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://perma.cc/M5DA-YSE6,
Finland,FIN,4.9  Can coercion be indirect?,Silent,,,,
Finland,FIN,4.10 Must the target state have knowledge of the coercive cyber operation constituting the purported intervention?,Silent,,,,
Finland,FIN,5. Does intervention include forms of persuasion or propaganda?,No,The requirement of coercion leaves out lesser forms of influence and persuasion that are commonplace in international relations.,International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://perma.cc/M5DA-YSE6,
Finland,FIN,6. Is there a de minimis exception to the prohibition?,Yes,"To be comparable to a real world intervention, cyber interference must also be of a serious nature.",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://perma.cc/M5DA-YSE6,
Finland,FIN,"7. Must the intervention be directed at matters over which the victim state is permitted by the principle of sovereignty, to decide freely?",Yes,...provided that it is done with the purpose of compelling or coercing that State in relation to affairs regarding which it has free choice (so-called domaine réservé).,International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://perma.cc/M5DA-YSE6,
Finland,FIN,7.1 Does the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives include only internal affairs?,No,"The limitation to sovereign affairs – such as a State’s political, economic or cultural system or the direction of its foreign policy – further distinguishes prohibited intervention from measures, the purpose of which is to compel another State to comply with its international obligations",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://perma.cc/M5DA-YSE6,
Finland,FIN,7.2 Does the prohibition proscribe intervention in a state’s external (foreign) affairs?,Yes,"The limitation to sovereign affairs – such as a State’s political, economic or cultural system or the direction of its foreign policy – further distinguishes prohibited intervention from measures, the purpose of which is to compel another State to comply with its international obligations.",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://perma.cc/M5DA-YSE6,
Finland,FIN,7.3 Is the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives referred to as domaine réservé?,Yes,...provided that it is done with the purpose of compelling or coercing that State in relation to affairs regarding which it has free choice (so-called domaine reserve),International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://perma.cc/M5DA-YSE6,
Finland,FIN,"7.4 Is the domaine réservé limited to matters involving the state’s choice of a political, economic, social, and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy?",Ambiguous,"...such as a State’s  political, economic or cultural system or the direction of its foreign policy.",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://perma.cc/M5DA-YSE6,
Finland,FIN,7.5 Does international law regulation over a matter of sovereign prerogative remove it from the domaine réservé?,Ambiguous,"The limitation to sovereign affairs – such as a State’s political, economic or cultural system or the direction of its foreign policy – further distinguishes prohibited intervention from measures, the purpose of which is to compel another State to comply with its international obligations",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://perma.cc/M5DA-YSE6,
Finland,FIN,7.6 Is critical infrastructure part of the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Finland,FIN,7.7 Are elections part of the domaine réservé?,Yes,"For instance, while the conduct of elections belongs undisputedly to the internal affairs of each State, all methods of electoral interference do not display the element of coercion. Hacking of voter databases or manipulation of vote counts in order to alter the election results has nevertheless been recognized as a fairly clear case.  To be comparable to a real world intervention, cyber interference must also be of a serious nature.",International Law and Cyberspace: Finland’s National Positions,https://perma.cc/M5DA-YSE6,
Finland,FIN,7.8 Is the operation of legislatures a part of the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Finland,FIN,7.9 Is financial system stability part of the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Finland,FIN,7.10 Are medical services part of the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
France,FRA,1. Is the prohibition of non-intervention a norm of customary international law?,Silent,,,,
France,FRA,2. Is the scope of the principle of non-intervention uncertain?,Silent,,,,
France,FRA,3. Does the prohibition of non-intervention apply to cyber-operations?,Yes,"§1.1. Many States are acquiring the capacity to prepare and conduct operations in cyberspace. When carried out to the detriment of the rights of other States, such operations may breach international law. Depending on the extent of their intrusion or their effects, they may violate the principles of sovereignty, non-intervention or even the prohibition of the threat or use of force.","International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace: Paper shared by France with the OEWG , pg. 2, §1.1 (2021).",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/France/France_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=D0XpLe,
