France,FRA,4.3 Does the deprivation of a State’s ability to control matters within its domaine réservé amount to coercion?,Silent,,,,
France,FRA,4.4 Does coercion extend to any affirmative acts designed to deprive another state of its freedom of choice with regard to a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?,Silent,,,,
France,FRA,4.5 Must there be a causal nexus between the responsible state’s actions/efforts and the effects on the target state’s internal or external affairs?,Silent,,,,
France,FRA,4.6 Can attempts to coerce an outcome amount to prohibited intervention?,Ambiguous,"§1.1. Depending on the extent of their intrusion or their effects, they may violate the principles of sovereignty, non-intervention or even the prohibition of the threat or use of force.  …. §1.1.1. ...interference which causes or may cause harm to France’s political, economic, social and cultural system, may constitute a violation of the principle of non-intervention","International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace: Paper shared by France with the OEWG , pg. 2, §§ 1.1 -1.1.1 (2021).",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/France/France_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=D0XpLe,
France,FRA,4.7 Is intent a constituent element of coercion?,Silent,,,,
France,FRA,"4.8 Must the action(s) constituting coercion be designed/intended to influence outcomes in, or conduct with respect to, a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?",Silent,,,,
France,FRA,4.9  Can coercion be indirect?,Silent,,,,
France,FRA,4.10 Must the target state have knowledge of the cyber operation constituting the purported intervention?,Silent,,,,
France,FRA,5. Does intervention include forms of persuasion or propaganda?,Silent,,,,
France,FRA,6. Is there a de minimis exception to the prohibition?,Silent,,,,
France,FRA,"7. Must the intervention be directed at matters over which the victim state is permitted by the principle of sovereignty, to decide freely?",Ambiguous,"§1.1.1. Interference by digital means in the internal or external affairs of France, International law applied to operations in cyberspace, i.e. interference which causes or may cause harm to France, International law applied to operations in cyberspace’s political, economic, social and cultural system, may constitute a violation of the principle of non-intervention.","International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace: Paper shared by France with the OEWG ,",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/France/France_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=D0XpLe,
France,FRA,7.1 Does the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives include only internal affairs?,No,"§1.1.1. Interference by digital means in the internal or external affairs of France, International law applied to operations in cyberspace, i.e. interference which causes or may cause harm to France, International law applied to operations in cyberspace’s political, economic, social and cultural system, may constitute a violation of the principle of non-intervention","International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace: Paper shared by France with the OEWG ,",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/France/France_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=D0XpLe,
France,FRA,7.2 Does the prohibition proscribe intervention in a state’s external (foreign) affairs?,Yes,"§1.1.1. Interference by digital means in the internal or external affairs of France, International law applied to operations in cyberspace, i.e. interference which causes or may cause harm to France, International law applied to operations in cyberspace’s political, economic, social and cultural system, may constitute a violation of the principle of non-intervention","International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace: Paper shared by France with the OEWG ,",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/France/France_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=D0XpLe,
France,FRA,7.3 Is the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives referred to as domaine réservé?,No,,,,
France,FRA,"7.4 Is the domaine réservé limited to matters involving the state’s choice of a political, economic, social, and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy?",Ambiguous,"§1.1.1. Interference by digital means in the internal or external affairs of France, International law applied to operations in cyberspace, i.e. interference which causes or may cause harm to France, International law applied to operations in cyberspace’s political, economic, social and cultural system, may constitute a violation of the principle of non-intervention","International Law Applied to Operations in Cyberspace: Paper shared by France with the OEWG ,",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/France/France_International%20Law%20and%20Cyberspace.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=D0XpLe,
