Germany,DEU,1. Is the prohibition of non-intervention a norm of customary international law?,Yes,"The prohibition of a wrongful intervention between States20 is not explicitly mentioned in the UN Charter. However, it is a corollary of the sovereignty principle, can be derived from art. 2 para. 1 UN Charter and is grounded in customary international law.",Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,
Germany,DEU,2. Is the scope of the principle of non-intervention uncertain?,Yes,"Generally, for State-attributable conduct to qualify as a wrongful intervention, the conduct must (1) interfere with the domaine réservé of a foreign State and (2) involve coercion. Especially the definition of the latter element requires further clarification in the cyber context.",Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,
Germany,DEU,3. Does the prohibition of non-intervention apply to cyber-operations?,Yes,Germany is of the opinion that cyber measures may constitute a prohibited intervention under international law if they are comparable in scale and effect to coercion in non-cyber contexts.,Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,
Germany,DEU,4. Is coercion a constituent element of prohibited intervention?,Yes,"Generally, for State-attributable conduct to qualify as a wrongful intervention, the conduct must (1) interfere with the domaine réservé of a foreign State and (2) involve coercion.",Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,
Germany,DEU,4.1 Is coercion limited to the employment of physical force?,No,"Malicious cyber activities will only in some cases amount to direct or indirect use of force. However, measures below this threshold may also qualify as coercive.",Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,
Germany,DEU,4.2 Can dictatorial demands or acts of compulsion or imposition constitute coercion?,Silent,,,,
Germany,DEU,4.3 Does the deprivation of a State’s ability to control matters within its domaine réservé amount to coercion?,Ambiguous,"Coercion implies that a State’s internal processes regarding aspects pertaining to its domaine réservé are significantly influenced or thwarted and that its will is manifestly bent by the foreign State’s conduct.  …it is conceivable that a State, by spreading disinformation via the internet, may deliberately incite violent political upheaval, riots and/or civil strife in a foreign country, thereby significantly impeding the orderly conduct of an election and the casting of ballots. Such activities may be comparable in scale and effect to the support of insurgents and may hence be akin to coercion in the above-mentioned sense.”",Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,
Germany,DEU,4.4 Does coercion extend to any affirmative acts designed to deprive another state of its freedom of choice with regard to a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?,Silent,,,,
Germany,DEU,4.5 Must there be a causal nexus between the responsible state’s actions/efforts and the effects on the target state’s internal or external affairs?,Silent,,,,
Germany,DEU,4.6 Can attempts to coerce an outcome amount to prohibited intervention?,Ambiguous,"Furthermore, beyond the mentioned examples, cyber activities targeting elections may be comparable in scale and effect to coercion if they aim at and result in a substantive disturbance or even permanent change of the political system of the targeted State, i.e. by significantly eroding public trust in a State’s political organs and processes, by seriously impeding important State organs in the fulfilment of their functions or by dissuading significant groups of citizens from voting, thereby undermining the meaningfulness of an election. Due to the complexity and singularity of such scenarios, it is difficult to formulate abstract criteria. Discussions in this context are still ongoing.",Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,
Germany,DEU,4.7 Is intent a constituent element of coercion?,Yes,"Moreover, the acting State must intend to intervene in the internal affairs of the target State – otherwise the scope of the non-intervention principle would be unduly broad.",Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,
Germany,DEU,"4.8 Must the action(s) constituting coercion be designed/intended to influence outcomes in, or conduct with respect to, a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?",Yes,"Moreover, the acting State must intend to intervene in the internal affairs of the target State otherwise the scope of the non-intervention principle would be unduly broad.",Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,
Germany,DEU,4.9   Can coercion be indirect?,Ambiguous,"However, measures below this threshold may also qualify as coercive. Generally, Germany is of the opinion that cyber measures may constitute a prohibited intervention under international law if they are comparable in scale and effect to coercion in non-cyber contexts.",Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,
Germany,DEU,4.10 Must the target state have knowledge of the coercive cyber operation constituting the purported intervention?,Silent,,,,
Germany,DEU,5. Does intervention include forms of persuasion or propaganda?,Yes,"Even harsher forms of communication such as pointed commentary and sharp criticism as well as (persistent) attempts to obtain, through discussion, a certain reaction or the performance of a certain measure from another State do not as such qualify as coercion.  …For example, it is conceivable that a State, by spreading disinformation via the internet, may deliberately incite violent political upheaval, riots and/or civil strife in a foreign country, thereby significantly impeding the orderly conduct of an election and the casting of ballots.",Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,
Germany,DEU,6. Is there a de minimis exception to the prohibition?,Silent,,,,
Germany,DEU,"7. Must the intervention be directed at matters over which the victim state is permitted by the principle of sovereignty, to decide freely?",Ambiguous,"State’s political independence is protected and it retains the right to freely choose its political, social, economic and cultural system. Inter alia, a State may   generally decide freely which role information and communication technologies should play in its governmental, administrative and adjudicative proceedings. Foreign interference in the conduct of elections of a State may under certain circumstances constitute a breach of sovereignty or, if pursued by means of coercion, of the prohibition of wrongful intervention",Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,
Germany,DEU,7.1 Does the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives include only internal affairs?,Yes,"Moreover, the acting State must intend to intervene in the internal affairs of the target State – otherwise the scope of the non-intervention principle would be unduly broad.",Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,
Germany,DEU,7.2 Does the prohibition proscribe intervention in a state’s external (foreign) affairs?,Ambiguous,"Moreover, the acting State must intend to intervene in the internal affairs of the target State – otherwise the scope of the non-intervention principle would be unduly broad.",Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,
Germany,DEU,7.3 Is the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives referred to as domaine réservé?,Yes,"Generally, for State-attributable conduct to qualify as a wrongful intervention, the conduct must (1) interfere with the domaine réservé of a foreign State",Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,
Germany,DEU,"7.4 Is the domaine réservé limited to matters involving the state’s choice of a political, economic, social, and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy?",Ambiguous,"By virtue of sovereignty, a State’s political independence is protected and it retains the right to freely choose its political, social, economic and cultural system. Inter alia, a State may generally decide freely which role information and communication technologies should play in its governmental, administrative and adjudicative proceedings.",Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,
Germany,DEU,Does international law regulation over a matter of sovereign prerogative remove it from the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Germany,DEU,7.6 Is critical infrastructure part of the domaine réservé?,Ambiguous,"By virtue of sovereignty, a State’s political independence is protected and it retains the right to freely choose its political, social, economic and cultural system. Inter alia, a State may generally decide freely which role information and communication technologies should play in its governmental, administrative and adjudicative proceedings.",Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,
Germany,DEU,7.7 Are elections part of the domaine réservé?,Yes,"Germany generally agrees with the opinion that malicious cyber activities targeting foreign elections may – either individually or as part of a wider campaign involving cyber and non-cyber-related tactics – constitute a wrongful intervention. Also, the disabling of election infrastructure and technology such as electronic ballots, etc. by malicious cyber activities may constitute a prohibited intervention, in particular if this compromises or even prevents the holding of an election, or if the results of an election are thereby substantially modified.",Germany on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/J9BX-UZ4U,
Germany,DEU,7.8 Is the operation of legislatures a part of the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Germany,DEU,7.9 Is financial system stability part of the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Germany,DEU,7.10 Are medical services part of the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
