Ireland,IRL,1. Is the prohibition of non-intervention a norm of customary international law?,Yes,"¶1. International law, including the Charter of the United Nations, applies in cyberspace. ¶8. The principle of non-intervention in the internal affairs of states, a corollary of the principle of sovereignty, involves the right of every sovereign state to conduct its affairs without interference.",Ireland Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/K2UL-4HYB,
Ireland,IRL,2. Is the scope of the principle of non-intervention uncertain?,Silent,,,,
Ireland,IRL,3. Does the prohibition of non-intervention apply to cyber-operations?,Yes,"¶8. The principle of non-intervention in the internal affairs of states, a corollary of the principle of sovereignty, involves the right of every sovereign state to conduct its affairs without interference. The principle of non-intervention, outside the context of use of force, applies to one state’s actions in relation to another state where two elements are present: (i) coercion by one state of another state; and (ii) in relation to ‘matters in which each state is permitted, by the principle of state sovereignty, to decide freely.’ ¶9. In order for the principle to be engaged, an intervention in the cyber context must be of sufficient seriousness, comparable in scale and effects to coercive action in a non-cyber context.",Ireland Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/K2UL-4HYB,
Ireland,IRL,4. Is coercion a constituent element of prohibited intervention?,Yes,"¶8. The principle of non-intervention, outside the context of use of force, applies to one state’s actions in relation to another state where two elements are present: (i) coercion by one state of another state; and (ii) in relation to ‘matters in which each state is permitted, by the principle of state sovereignty, to decide freely.’",Ireland Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/K2UL-4HYB,
Ireland,IRL,4.1 Is coercion limited to the employment of physical force?,Ambiguous,"¶8. The principle of non-intervention, outside the context of use of force, applies to one state’s actions in relation to another state where two elements are present: (i) coercion by one state of another state; and (ii) in relation to ‘matters in which each state is permitted, by the principle of state sovereignty, to decide freely.’",Ireland Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/K2UL-4HYB,
Ireland,IRL,4.2 Can dictatorial demands or acts of compulsion or imposition constitute coercion?,Silent,,,,
Ireland,IRL,4.3 Does the deprivation of a State’s ability to control matters within its domaine réservé amount to coercion?,Silent,,,,
Ireland,IRL,4.4 Does coercion extend to any affirmative acts designed to deprive another state of its freedom of choice with regard to a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?,Ambiguous,"¶8. The principle of non-intervention, outside the context of use of force, applies to one state’s actions in relation to another state where two elements are present: (i) coercion by one state of another state; and (ii) in relation to ‘matters in which each state is permitted, by the principle of state sovereignty, to decide freely.’ As regards what is encompassed by the latter element, the ICJ in the Nicaragua case provided specific examples such as the ‘choice of a political, economic, social and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy.’",Ireland Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/K2UL-4HYB,
Ireland,IRL,4.5 Must there be a causal nexus between the responsible state’s actions/efforts and the effects on the target state’s internal or external affairs?,Silent,,,,
Ireland,IRL,4.6  Can attempts to coerce an outcome amount to prohibited intervention?,Silent,,,,
Ireland,IRL,4.7 Is intent a constituent element of coercion?,Silent,,,,
Ireland,IRL,"4.8 Must the action(s) constituting coercion be designed/intended to influence outcomes in, or conduct with respect to, a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?",Silent,,,,
Ireland,IRL,4.9  Can coercion be indirect?,Silent,,,,
Ireland,IRL,4.10 Must the target state have knowledge of the coercive cyber operation constituting the purported intervention?,Silent,,,,
Ireland,IRL,5. Does intervention include forms of persuasion or propaganda?,Ambiguous,"¶10. Unlawful interventions should be distinguished from lawful forms of influence and pressure on states, such as lobbying governments or unfriendly acts. Likewise, they do not include countermeasures permitted under international law to induce a state to comply with its obligations on foot of an internationally wrongful act.",Ireland Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/K2UL-4HYB,
Ireland,IRL,6. Is there a de minimis exception to the prohibition?,Yes,"¶9. In order for the principle to be engaged, an intervention in the cyber context must be of sufficient seriousness, comparable in scale and effects to coercive action in a non-cyber context. ¶10. Unlawful interventions should be distinguished from lawful forms of influence and pressure on states, such as lobbying governments or unfriendly acts. Likewise, they do not include countermeasures permitted under international law to induce a state to comply with its obligations on foot of an internationally wrongful act.",Ireland Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/K2UL-4HYB,
Ireland,IRL,"7. Must the intervention be directed at matters over which the victim state is permitted by the principle of sovereignty, to decide freely?",Yes,"¶8. The principle of non-intervention, outside the context of use of force, applies to one state’s actions in relation to another state where two elements are present: (i) coercion by one state of another state; and (ii) in relation to ‘matters in which each state is permitted, by the principle of state sovereignty, to decide freely.’",Ireland Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/K2UL-4HYB,
Ireland,IRL,7.1 Does the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives include only internal affairs?,No,"¶8. As regards what is encompassed by the latter element, the ICJ in the Nicaragua case provided specific examples such as the ‘choice of a political, economic, social and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy.’ This is often referred to as the domaine réservé of a state.",Ireland Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/K2UL-4HYB,
Ireland,IRL,7.2 Does the prohibition proscribe intervention in a state’s external (foreign) affairs?,Yes,"¶8. As regards what is encompassed by the latter element, the ICJ in the Nicaragua case provided specific examples such as the ‘choice of a political, economic, social and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy.’ This is often referred to as the domaine réservé of a state.",Ireland Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/K2UL-4HYB,
Ireland,IRL,7.3 Is the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives referred to as domaine réservé?,Yes,"¶8. As regards what is encompassed by the latter element, the ICJ in the Nicaragua case provided specific examples such as the ‘choice of a political, economic, social and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy.’ This is often referred to as the domaine réservé of a state.",Ireland Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/K2UL-4HYB,
Ireland,IRL,"7.4 Is the domaine réservé limited to matters involving the state’s choice of a political, economic, social, and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy?",Ambiguous,"¶8. As regards what is encompassed by the latter element, the ICJ in the Nicaragua case provided specific examples such as the ‘choice of a political, economic, social and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy.’ This is often referred to as the domaine réservé of a state.",Ireland Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/K2UL-4HYB,
Ireland,IRL,7.5 Does international law regulation over a matter of sovereign prerogative remove it from the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Ireland,IRL,7.6 Is critical infrastructure part of the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Ireland,IRL,7.7 Are elections part of the domaine réservé?,Yes,"¶8. As regards what is encompassed by the latter element, the ICJ in the Nicaragua case provided specific examples such as the ‘choice of a political, economic, social and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy.’ This is often referred to as the domaine réservé of a state. ¶9. For instance, malicious cyber-operations seriously compromising healthcare systems or national elections are capable of amounting to unlawful interventions.",Ireland Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/K2UL-4HYB,
Ireland,IRL,7.8 Is the operation of legislatures a part of the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Ireland,IRL,7.9 Is financial system stability part of the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Ireland,IRL,7.10 Are medical services part of the domaine réservé?,Yes,"¶8. As regards what is encompassed by the latter element, the ICJ in the Nicaragua case provided specific examples such as the ‘choice of a political, economic, social and culturalsystem, and the formulation of foreign policy.’ This is often referred to as the domaine réservé of a state. ¶9. For instance, malicious cyber-operations seriously compromising healthcare systems or national elections are capable of amounting to unlawful interventions.",Ireland Position Paper on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/K2UL-4HYB,
Israel,ISR,1. Is the prohibition of non-intervention a norm of customary international law?,Silent,,,,
Israel,ISR,2. Is the scope of the principle of non-intervention uncertain?,Silent,,,,
Israel,ISR,3. Does the prohibition of non-intervention apply to cyber-operations?,Yes,"¶7 . “We concur with the various positions expressed in this regard, such as that which was presented by former U.S. State Department Legal Adviser Brian J. Egan, and more recently reiterated by U.S. Department of Defense General Counsel Paul C. Ney Jr., that a ‘cyber operation by a State that interferes with another country’s ability to hold an election or that manipulates another country’s election results would be a clear violation of the rule of non-intervention.’”",Israel’s Perspective on Key Legal and Practical Issues Concerning the Application of International Law to Cyber Operations,https://perma.cc/3ENJ-5H3N,
Israel,ISR,4. Is coercion a constituent element of prohibited intervention?,Ambiguous,"¶6. “It has been taken to mean that State A cannot take actions to “coerce” State B in pursuing a course of action, or refraining from a course of action, in matters pertaining to State B’s core internal affairs, such as its economic or foreign policy choices. Its traditional application has focused on military intervention and support to armed groups seeking the overthrow of the regime in another State. This could presumably also relate to support given to armed groups in the cyber domain, such as providing information regarding cyber vulnerabilities of the State.”",Israel’s Perspective on Key Legal and Practical Issues Concerning the Application of International Law to Cyber Operations,https://perma.cc/3ENJ-5H3N,
Israel,ISR,4.1 Is coercion limited to the employment of physical force?,No,"¶6. “This could presumably also relate to support given to armed groups in the cyber domain, such as providing information regarding cyber vulnerabilities of the State.”  ¶7. “We concur with the various positions expressed in this regard...that a ‘cyber operation by a State that interferes with another country’s ability to hold an election or that manipulates another country’s election results would be a clear violation of the rule of non-intervention.’”",Israel’s Perspective on Key Legal and Practical Issues Concerning the Application of International Law to Cyber Operations,https://perma.cc/3ENJ-5H3N,
