Netherlands,NLD,1. Is the prohibition of non-intervention a norm of customary international law?,Silent,,,,
Netherlands,NLD,2. Is the scope of the principle of non-intervention uncertain?,Yes,"“The precise definition of coercion, and thus of unauthorised intervention, has not yet fully crystallized in international law.”","Government of the Kingdom of the Netherlands, Appendix: International law in cyberspace",https://perma.cc/YFD4-LB25,
Netherlands,NLD,3. Does the prohibition of non-intervention apply to cyber-operations?,Yes,In this appendix the government will discuss a number of significant rules of international law that apply to states in cyberspace.,"Government of the Kingdom of the Netherlands, Appendix: International law in cyberspace",https://perma.cc/YFD4-LB25,
Netherlands,NLD,4. Is coercion a constituent element of prohibited intervention?,Yes,Intervention is defined as interference in the internal or external affairs of another state with a view to employing coercion against that state.,"Government of the Kingdom of the Netherlands, Appendix: International law in cyberspace",https://perma.cc/YFD4-LB25,
Netherlands,NLD,4.1 Is coercion limited to the employment of physical force?,Ambiguous,"Although there is no clear definition of the element of coercion, it should be noted that the use of force will always meet the definition of coercion”, which indicates that the category of coercion is broader than just the use of force.","Government of the Kingdom of the Netherlands, Appendix: International law in cyberspace",https://perma.cc/YFD4-LB25,
Netherlands,NLD,4.2 Can dictatorial demands or acts of compulsion or imposition constitute coercion?,Yes,"The precise definition of coercion, and thus of unauthorised intervention, has not yet fully crystallised in international law. In essence it means compelling a state to take a course of action (whether an act or an omission) that it would not otherwise voluntarily pursue.","Government of the Kingdom of the Netherlands, Appendix: International law in cyberspace",https://perma.cc/YFD4-LB25,
Netherlands,NLD,4.3 Does the deprivation of a State’s ability to control matters within its domaine réservé amount to coercion?,Silent,,,,
Netherlands,NLD,4.4 Does coercion extend to any affirmative acts designed to deprive another state of its freedom of choice with regard to a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?,Ambiguous,"The precise definition of coercion, and thus of unauthorised intervention, has not yet fully crystallised in international law. In essence it means compelling a state to take a course of action (whether an act or an omission) that it would not otherwise voluntarily pursue.","Government of the Kingdom of the Netherlands, Appendix: International law in cyberspace",https://perma.cc/YFD4-LB25,
Netherlands,NLD,4.5 Must there be a causal nexus between the responsible state’s actions/efforts and the effects on the target state’s internal or external affairs?,Silent,,,,
Netherlands,NLD,4.6  Can attempts to coerce an outcome amount to prohibited intervention?,Silent,,,,
Netherlands,NLD,4.7 Is intent a constituent element of coercion?,Yes,The goal of the intervention must be to effect change in the behaviour of the target state.,"Government of the Kingdom of the Netherlands, Appendix: International law in cyberspace",https://perma.cc/YFD4-LB25,
Netherlands,NLD,"4.8 Must the action(s) constituting coercion be designed/intended to influence outcomes in, or conduct with respect to, a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?",Yes,"The precise definition of coercion, and thus of unauthorised intervention, has not yet fully crystallised in international law. In essence it means compelling a state to take a course of action (whether an act or an omission) that it would not otherwise voluntarily pursue. The goal of the intervention must be to effect change in the behaviour of the target state.","Government of the Kingdom of the Netherlands, Appendix: International law in cyberspace",https://perma.cc/YFD4-LB25,
Netherlands,NLD,4.9  Can coercion be indirect?,Silent,,,,
Netherlands,NLD,4.10 Must the target state have knowledge of the coercive cyber operation constituting the purported intervention?,Silent,,,,
Netherlands,NLD,5. Does intervention include forms of persuasion or propaganda?,Silent,,,,
Netherlands,NLD,6. Is there a de minimis exception to the prohibition?,Silent,,,,
Netherlands,NLD,"7. Must the intervention be directed at matters over which the victim state is permitted by the principle of sovereignty, to decide freely?",Yes,"Intervention is defined as interference in the internal or external affairs of another state with a view to employing coercion against that state. Such affairs concern matters over which, in accordance with the principle of sovereignty, states themselves have exclusive authority.","Government of the Kingdom of the Netherlands, Appendix: International law in cyberspace",https://perma.cc/YFD4-LB25,
Netherlands,NLD,7.1 Does the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives include only internal affairs?,No,"Intervention is defined as interference in the internal or external affairs of another state with a view to employing coercion against that state. Such affairs concern matters over which, in accordance with the principle of sovereignty, states themselves have exclusive authority.","Government of the Kingdom of the Netherlands, Appendix: International law in cyberspace",https://perma.cc/YFD4-LB25,
Netherlands,NLD,7.2 Does the prohibition proscribe intervention in a state’s external (foreign) affairs?,Yes,"Intervention is defined as interference in the internal or external affairs of another state with a view to employing coercion against that state. Such affairs concern matters over which, in accordance with the principle of sovereignty, states themselves have exclusive authority.","Government of the Kingdom of the Netherlands, Appendix: International law in cyberspace",https://perma.cc/YFD4-LB25,
Netherlands,NLD,7.3 Is the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives referred to as domaine réservé?,No,,,,
Netherlands,NLD,"7.4 Is the domaine réservé limited to matters involving the state’s choice of a political, economic, social, and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy?",Silent,,,,
Netherlands,NLD,7.5 Does international law regulation over a matter of sovereign prerogative remove it from the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Netherlands,NLD,7.6 Is critical infrastructure part of the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Netherlands,NLD,7.7 Are elections part of the domaine réservé?,Yes,National elections are an example of internal affairs. The recognition of states and membership of international organisations are examples of external affairs.,"Government of the Kingdom of the Netherlands, Appendix: International law in cyberspace",https://perma.cc/YFD4-LB25,
Netherlands,NLD,7.8 Is the operation of legislatures a part of the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Netherlands,NLD,7.9 Is financial system stability part of the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Netherlands,NLD,7.10 Are medical services part of the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
New Zealand,NZL,1. Is the prohibition of non-intervention a norm of customary international law?,Silent,,,,
New Zealand,NZL,2. Is the scope of the principle of non-intervention uncertain?,Silent,,,,
New Zealand,NZL,3. Does the prohibition of non-intervention apply to cyber-operations?,Yes,"¶9.  “Malicious state cyber activity may be inconsistent with the rule of non-intervention. Such activity will violate the rule of non-intervention if it: a. has significant effects on a matter which falls within the target state’s inherently sovereign functions / domaine réservé (e.g. the right freely to choose its political, economic, social and cultural system, or matters such as taxation, national security, policing, border control, and the formulation of foreign policy); and b. is coercive (i.e. there is an intention to deprive the target state of control over matters falling within the scope of its inherently sovereign functions). Coercion can be direct or indirect and may range from dictatorial threats to more subtle means of control. While the coercive intention of the state actor is a critical element of the rule, intention may in some circumstances be inferred from the effects of cyber activity.”",The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/5FBX-CJ58,
New Zealand,NZL,4. Is coercion a constituent element of prohibited intervention?,Yes,"¶9b. is coercive (i.e. there is an intention to deprive the target state of control over matters falling within the scope of its inherently sovereign functions). Coercion can be direct or indirect and may range from dictatorial threats to more subtle means of control. While the coercive intention of the state actor is a critical element of the rule, intention may in some circumstances be inferred from the effects of cyber activity.",The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/5FBX-CJ58,
New Zealand,NZL,4.1 Is coercion limited to the employment of physical force?,No,"¶9b. is coercive (i.e. there is an intention to deprive the target state of control over matters falling within the scope of its inherently sovereign functions). Coercion can be direct or indirect and may range from dictatorial threats to more subtle means of control. While the coercive intention of the state actor is a critical element of the rule, intention may in some circumstances be inferred from the effects of cyber activity.",The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/5FBX-CJ58,
