New Zealand,NZL,7.1 Does the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives include only internal affairs?,No,"¶9a. has significant effects on a matter which falls within the target state’s inherently sovereign functions / domaine réservé (e.g. the right freely to choose its political, economic, social and cultural system, or matters such as taxation, national security, policing, border control, and the formulation of foreign policy);",The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/5FBX-CJ58,
New Zealand,NZL,7.2 Does the prohibition proscribe intervention in a state’s external (foreign) affairs?,Yes,"¶9a. has significant effects on a matter which falls within the target state’s inherently sovereign functions / domaine réservé (e.g. the right freely to choose its political, economic, social and cultural system, or matters such as taxation, national security, policing, border control, and the formulation of foreign policy);",The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/5FBX-CJ58,
New Zealand,NZL,7.3 Is the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives referred to as domaine réservé?,Yes,"¶9a. has significant effects on a matter which falls within the target state’s inherently sovereign functions / domaine réservé (e.g. the right freely to choose its political, economic, social and cultural system, or matters such as taxation, national security, policing, border control, and the formulation of foreign policy);",The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/5FBX-CJ58,
New Zealand,NZL,"7.4 Is the domaine réservé limited to matters involving the state’s choice of a political, economic, social, and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy?",No,"¶9. Malicious state cyber activity may be inconsistent with the rule of non-intervention. Such activity will violate the rule of non-intervention if it: a. has significant effects on a matter which falls within the target state’s inherently sovereign functions / domaine réservé (e.g. the right freely to choose its political, economic, social and cultural system, or matters such as taxation, national security, policing, border control, and the formulation of foreign policy);...”",The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/5FBX-CJ58,
New Zealand,NZL,7.5 Does international law regulation over a matter of sovereign prerogative remove it from the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
New Zealand,NZL,7.6 Is critical infrastructure part of the domaine réservé?,Yes,"¶10. Examples of malicious cyber activity that might violate the non-intervention rule include: a cyber operation that deliberately manipulates the vote tally in an election or deprives a significant part of the electorate of the ability to vote; a prolonged and coordinated cyber disinformation operation that significantly undermines a state’s public health efforts during a pandemic; and cyber activity deliberately causing significant damage to, or loss of functionality in, a state’s critical infrastructure, including – for example – its healthcare system, financial system, or its electricity or telecommunications network.",The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/5FBX-CJ58,
New Zealand,NZL,7.7 Are elections part of the domaine réservé?,Yes,¶10 . Examples of malicious cyber activity that might violate the non-intervention rule include: a cyber operation that deliberately manipulates the vote tally in an election or deprives a significant part of the electorate of the ability to vote;...,The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/5FBX-CJ58,
New Zealand,NZL,7.8 Is the operation of legislatures a part of the domaine réservé?,Ambiguous,"¶9a. has significant effects on a matter which falls within the target state’s inherently sovereign functions / domaine réservé (e.g. the right freely to choose its political, economic, social and cultural system, or matters such as taxation, national security, policing, border control, and the formulation of foreign policy);",The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/5FBX-CJ58,
New Zealand,NZL,7.9 Is financial system stability part of the domaine réservé?,Yes,"¶10....and cyber activity deliberately causing significant damage to, or loss of functionality in, a state’s critical infrastructure, including – for example – its healthcare system, financial system, or its electricity or telecommunications network.",The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/5FBX-CJ58,
New Zealand,NZL,7.10 Are medical services part of the domaine réservé?,Yes,"¶10. Examples of malicious cyber activity...; a prolonged and coordinated cyber disinformation operation that significantly undermines a state’s public health efforts during a pandemic; and cyber activity deliberately causing significant damage to, or loss of functionality in, a state’s critical infrastructure, including – for example – its healthcare system, financial system, or its electricity or telecommunications network.”",The Application of International Law to State Activity in Cyberspace,https://perma.cc/5FBX-CJ58,
Norway,NOR,1. Is the prohibition of non-intervention a norm of customary international law?,Yes,"“Accordingly, a State must not carry out cyber operations in breach of the prohibition of intervention, according to customary international law.""",Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace,https://www.regjeringen.no/contentassets/a8911fc020c94eb386a1ec7917bf0d03/norwegian_positions.pdf,
Norway,NOR,2. Is the scope of the principle of non-intervention uncertain?,Ambiguous,"Norway recognises that no State seems to object to the application of the prohibition of intervention ... in the cyber context. ... However, Norway is aware that there are differences of opinion as to where the threshold for breach lies.”",Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace,https://www.regjeringen.no/contentassets/a8911fc020c94eb386a1ec7917bf0d03/norwegian_positions.pdf,
Norway,NOR,3. Does the prohibition of non-intervention apply to cyber-operations?,Yes,“The prohibition of intervention applies to a State’s cyber operations as it does to other State activities.”,Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Norway,NOR,4. Is coercion a constituent element of prohibited intervention?,Yes,“The constituent element of coercion means that cyber activities that are merely influential or persuasive will not qualify as illegal intervention.”,Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Norway,NOR,4.1 Is coercion limited to the employment of physical force?,No,"“A cyber operation must therefore not be carried out to compel the target State to take a course of action, whether by act or omission, in a way that it would not otherwise voluntarily have pursued (coercion) in matters relating to its internal or external affairs (domaine réservé) – such as a State’s political, economic, social or cultural system or the formulation of its foreign policy.”",Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Norway,NOR,4.2 Can dictatorial demands or acts of compulsion or imposition constitute coercion?,Yes,"Cyber operations that compel the target State to take a course of action, whether by act or omission, in a way that it would not otherwise voluntarily have pursued (coercion) in matters relating to its internal or external affairs (domaine réservé), will constitute an intervention in violation of international law",Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace,https://www.regjeringen.no/contentassets/a8911fc020c94eb386a1ec7917bf0d03/norwegian_positions.pdf,
Norway,NOR,4.3 Does the deprivation of a State’s ability to control matters within its domaine réservé amount to coercion?,Yes,"“A cyber operation must therefore not be carried out to compel the target State to take a course of action, whether by act or omission, in a way that it would not otherwise voluntarily have pursued (coercion) in matters relating to its internal or external affairs (domaine réservé) – such as a State’s political, economic, social or cultural system or the formulation of its foreign policy.”",Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Norway,NOR,4.4 Does coercion extend to any affirmative acts designed to deprive another state of its freedom of choice with regard to a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?,Silent,,,,
Norway,NOR,4.5 Must there be a causal nexus between the responsible state’s actions/efforts and the effects on the target state’s internal or external affairs?,Silent,,,,
Norway,NOR,4.6 Can attempts to coerce an outcome amount to prohibited intervention?,Ambiguous,"“Thus, carrying out cyber operations with the intent of altering election results in another State, for example by manipulating election systems or unduly influencing public opinion through the dissemination of confidential information obtained through cyber operations (‘hack and leak’), would be in violation of the prohibition of intervention.”",Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Norway,NOR,4.7 Is intent a constituent element of coercion?,Silent,,,,
Norway,NOR,"4.8 Must the action(s) constituting coercion be designed/intended to influence outcomes in, or conduct with respect to, a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?",Silent,,,,
Norway,NOR,4.9  Can coercion be indirect?,Silent,,,,
Norway,NOR,4.10 Must the target state have knowledge of the coercive cyber operation constituting the purported intervention?,Silent,,,,
Norway,NOR,5. Does intervention include forms of persuasion or propaganda?,No,“The constituent element of coercion means that cyber activities that are merely influential or persuasive will not qualify as illegal intervention.”,Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Norway,NOR,6. Is there a de minimis exception to the prohibition?,Silent,,,,
Norway,NOR,"7. Must the intervention be directed at matters over which the victim state is permitted by the principle of sovereignty, to decide freely?",Silent,,,,
Norway,NOR,7.1 Does the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives include only internal affairs?,No,"“A cyber operation must therefore not be carried out to compel the target State to take a course of action, whether by act or omission, in a way that it would not otherwise voluntarily have pursued (coercion) in matters relating to its internal or external affairs (domaine réservé)”",Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Norway,NOR,7.2 Does the prohibition proscribe intervention in a state’s external (foreign) affairs?,Yes,"“A cyber operation must therefore not be carried out to compel the target State to take a course of action, whether by act or omission, in a way that it would not otherwise voluntarily have pursued (coercion) in matters relating to its internal or external affairs (domaine réservé)”",Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Norway,NOR,7.3 Is the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives referred to as domaine réservé?,Yes,“...matters relating to its internal or external affairs (domaine réservé)”,Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Norway,NOR,"7.4 Is the domaine réservé limited to matters involving the state’s choice of a political, economic, social, and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy?",Ambiguous,"“...matters relating to its internal or external affairs (domaine réservé) – such as a State’s political, economic, social or cultural system or the formulation of its foreign policy.”",Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Norway,NOR,7.5 Does international law regulation over a matter of sovereign prerogative remove it from the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Norway,NOR,7.6 Is critical infrastructure part of the domaine réservé?,Yes,“...would be in violation of the prohibition of intervention. Another example is a cyber operation deliberately causing a temporary shutdown of the target State’s critical infrastructure...”,Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Norway,NOR,7.7 Are elections part of the domaine réservé?,Yes,“Holding elections is an example of a matter within a State’s domaine réservé.”,Norwegian Positions on Selected Questions of International Law Relating to Cyberspace UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
