Poland,POL,1. Is the prohibition of non-intervention a norm of customary international law?,Silent,,,,
Poland,POL,2. Is the scope of the principle of non-intervention uncertain?,Yes,"“To be in breach of international law, an intervention must include the element of coercion that aims at influencing the state’s decisions belonging to its domaine réservé, i.e. the area of state activity that remains its exclusive competence under the principle of sovereignty.”

“There is no universally acceptable definition of ‘coercion’”","The Republic of Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, §3 (2022).",https://perma.cc/EN4F-N5QV,
Poland,POL,3. Does the prohibition of non-intervention apply to cyber-operations?,Yes,"“A cyber operation that adversely affects the functioning and security of the political, economic, military or social system of a state, potentially leading to the state‘s conduct that would not occur otherwise, may be considered a prohibited intervention.”","The Republic of Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, §3 (2022).",https://perma.cc/EN4F-N5QV,
Poland,POL,4. Is coercion a constituent element of prohibited intervention?,Yes,"“To be in breach of international law, an intervention must include the element of coercion...”","The Republic of Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, §3 (2022).",https://perma.cc/EN4F-N5QV,
Poland,POL,4.1 Is coercion limited to the employment of physical force?,No,"“To be in breach of international law, an intervention must include the element of coercion that aims at influencing the state’s decisions belonging to its domaine réservé”

“A cyber operation that adversely affects the functioning and security of the political, economic, military or social system of a state, potentially leading to the state‘s conduct that would not occur otherwise, may be considered a prohibited intervention.”

“In particular, any action in cyberspace that would prevent the filing of tax returns online or any interference with ICT systems that would prevent a reliable and timely conduct of democratic elections would be a violation of international law.”","The Republic of Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, §3 (2022).",https://perma.cc/EN4F-N5QV,
Poland,POL,4.2 Can dictatorial demands or acts of compulsion or imposition constitute coercion?,Silent,,,,
Poland,POL,4.3 Does the deprivation of a State’s ability to control matters within its domaine réservé amount to coercion?,Yes,"“To be in breach of international law, an intervention must include the element of coercion that aims at influencing the state’s decisions belonging to its domaine réservé, i.e. the area of state activity that remains its exclusive competence under the principle of sovereignty.”","The Republic of Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, §3 (2022).",https://perma.cc/EN4F-N5QV,
Poland,POL,4.4 Does coercion extend to any affirmative acts designed to deprive another state of its freedom of choice with regard to a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?,Yes,"“A cyber operation that adversely affects the functioning and security of the political, economic, military or social system of a state, potentially leading to the state‘s conduct that would not occur otherwise, may be considered a prohibited intervention.”","The Republic of Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, §3 (2022).",https://perma.cc/EN4F-N5QV,
Poland,POL,4.5 Must there be a causal nexus between the responsible state’s actions/efforts and the effects on the target state’s internal or external affairs?,Silent,,,,
Poland,POL,4.6 Can attempts to coerce an outcome amount to prohibited intervention?,Yes,"“A cyber operation that adversely affects the functioning and security of the political, economic, military or social system of a state, potentially leading to the state‘s conduct that would not occur otherwise, may be considered a prohibited intervention.”","The Republic of Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, §3 (2022).",https://perma.cc/EN4F-N5QV,
Poland,POL,4.7 Is intent a constituent element of coercion?,Silent,,,,
Poland,POL,"4.8 Must the action(s) constituting coercion be designed/intended to influence outcomes in, or conduct with respect to, a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?",Yes,"“To be in breach of international law, an intervention must include the element of coercion that aims at influencing the state’s decisions belonging to its domaine réservé, i.e. the area of state activity that remains its exclusive competence under the principle of sovereignty.”","The Republic of Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, §3 (2022).",https://perma.cc/EN4F-N5QV,
Poland,POL,4.9  Can coercion be indirect?,Silent,,,,
Poland,POL,4.10 Must the target state have knowledge of the coercive cyber operation constituting the purported intervention?,Silent,,,,
Poland,POL,5. Does intervention include forms of persuasion or propaganda?,Ambiguous,"“It should also be noted that a wide-scale and targeted disinformation campaign may also contravene the principle of non-intervention, in particular when it results in civil unrest that requires specific responses on the part of the state.”","The Republic of Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, §3 (2022).",https://perma.cc/EN4F-N5QV,
Poland,POL,6. Is there a de minimis exception to the prohibition?,Silent,,,,
Poland,POL,"7. Must the intervention be directed at matters over which the victim state is permitted by the principle of sovereignty, to decide freely?",Yes,"“To be in breach of international law, an intervention must include the element of coercion that aims at influencing the state’s decisions belonging to its domaine réservé, i.e. the area of state activity that remains its exclusive competence under the principle of sovereignty.”","The Republic of Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, §3 (2022).",https://perma.cc/EN4F-N5QV,
Poland,POL,7.1 Does the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives include only internal affairs?,No,“Intervention in internal or external affairs of another state that fall under its domestic jurisdiction is an action that contravenes international law.”,"The Republic of Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, §3 (2022).",https://perma.cc/EN4F-N5QV,
Poland,POL,7.2 Does the prohibition proscribe intervention in a state’s external (foreign) affairs?,Yes,“Intervention in internal or external affairs of another state that fall under its domestic jurisdiction is an action that contravenes international law.”,"The Republic of Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, §3 (2022).",https://perma.cc/EN4F-N5QV,
Poland,POL,7.3 Is the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives referred to as domaine réservé?,Yes,"“...belonging to its domaine réservé, i.e. the area of state activity that remains its exclusive competence under the principle of sovereignty.”","The Republic of Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, §3 (2022).",https://perma.cc/EN4F-N5QV,
Poland,POL,"7.4 Is the domaine réservé limited to matters involving the state’s choice of a political, economic, social, and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy?",No,"“A cyber operation that adversely affects the functioning and security of the political, economic, military or social system of a state, potentially leading to the state‘s conduct that would not occur otherwise, may be considered a prohibited intervention.”","The Republic of Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, §3 (2022).",https://perma.cc/EN4F-N5QV,
Poland,POL,7.5 Does international law regulation over a matter of sovereign prerogative remove it from the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Poland,POL,7.6 Is critical infrastructure part of the domaine réservé?,Ambiguous,“...the Republic of Poland takes the position that the violation of a state‘s sovereignty may occur both in the event of an attack against state infrastructure and against private infrastructure.”,"The Republic of Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, §3 (2022).",https://perma.cc/EN4F-N5QV,
Poland,POL,7.7 Are elections part of the domaine réservé?,Yes,"“To be in breach of international law, an intervention must include the element of coercion that aims at influencing the state’s decisions belonging to its domaine réservé,...” “A cyber operation that adversely affects the functioning and security of the political, economic, military or social system of a state, potentially leading to the state‘s conduct that would not occur otherwise, may be considered a prohibited intervention. In particular, any action in cyberspace that would prevent the filing of tax returns online or any interference with ICT systems that would prevent a reliable and timely conduct of democratic elections would be a violation of international law.”","The Republic of Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, §3 (2022).",https://perma.cc/EN4F-N5QV,
Poland,POL,7.8 Is the operation of legislatures a part of the domaine réservé?,Yes,"“Similarly, depriving the parliament working remotely of the possibility of voting online to adopt a law or modifying the outcome of such voting would also be such a violation.”","The Republic of Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, §3 (2022).",https://perma.cc/EN4F-N5QV,
Poland,POL,7.9 Is financial system stability part of the domaine réservé?,Ambiguous,"“A cyber operation that adversely affects the functioning and security of the political, economic, military or social system of a state, potentially leading to the state‘s conduct that would not occur otherwise, may be considered a prohibited intervention.”","The Republic of Poland’s Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, §3 (2022).",https://perma.cc/EN4F-N5QV,
Poland,POL,7.10 Are medical services part of the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Romania,ROU,1. Is the prohibition of non-intervention a norm of customary international law?,Yes,"“According to international law, States are under the duty not to intervene in matters within the domestic jurisdiction of any State, in accordance with the Charter...”",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Romania,ROU,2. Is the scope of the principle of non-intervention uncertain?,Silent,,,,
Romania,ROU,3. Does the prohibition of non-intervention apply to cyber-operations?,Yes,"“Romania is of the strong opinion that existing international law equally applies to cyberspace..."" ""Therefore, depending on the situation, interference in the internal or external  affairs of Romania (that is interference which causes or may cause harm to Romania’s economic, political, social and/ or cultural system) may constitute a violation of the principle of non-intervention""",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Romania,ROU,4. Is coercion a constituent element of prohibited intervention?,Yes,"“In order for such intervention to be illegal under international law, it must be coerced, meaning that the goal of the intervention must be to effectively change the behavior of the target State...”",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Romania,ROU,4.1 Is coercion limited to the employment of physical force?,Silent,,,,
Romania,ROU,4.2 Can dictatorial demands or acts of compulsion or imposition constitute coercion?,Silent,,,,
Romania,ROU,4.3 Does the deprivation of a State’s ability to control matters within its domaine réservé amount to coercion?,Ambiguous,"“In other words, the following criteria must be met in order for an act to qualify as prohibited intervention under international law:  the act must bear on those matters in which States may decide freely (internal and external affairs – the domain reservé of States)...”",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Romania,ROU,4.4 Does coercion extend to any affirmative acts designed to deprive another state of its freedom of choice with regard to a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?,Silent,,,,
Romania,ROU,4.5 Must there be a causal nexus between the responsible state’s actions/efforts and the effects on the target state’s internal or external affairs?,Yes,“...there has to be a causal nexus between the coercive act and the effect on the internal or external affairs of the target State.”,UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Romania,ROU,4.6 Can attempts to coerce an outcome amount to prohibited intervention?,Ambiguous,"“In other words, the following criteria must be met in order for an act to qualify as prohibited intervention under international law: ✓ the act must bear on those matters in which States may decide freely (internal and external affairs – the domain reservé of States); ✓ the act must be coercive in nature; ✓ there has to be a causal nexus between the coercive act and the effect on the internal or external affairs of the target State.”",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Romania,ROU,4.7 Is intent a constituent element of coercion?,Yes,"“In order for such intervention to be illegal under international law, it must be coerced, meaning that the goal of the intervention must be to effectively change the behavior of the target State; the incidence of coercion must be assessed on a case-by-case basis, in order to determine the violation of the principle of non-intervention.”",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Romania,ROU,"4.8 Must the action(s) constituting coercion be designed/intended to influence outcomes in, or conduct with respect to, a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?",Silent,,,,
Romania,ROU,4.9  Can coercion be indirect?,Silent,,,,
Romania,ROU,4.10 Must the target state have knowledge of the coercive cyber operation constituting the purported intervention?,Silent,,,,
Romania,ROU,5. Does intervention include forms of persuasion or propaganda?,Silent,,,,
Romania,ROU,6. Is there a de minimis exception to the prohibition?,Silent,,,,
Romania,ROU,"7. Must the intervention be directed at matters over which the victim state is permitted by the principle of sovereignty, to decide freely?",Yes,“...the act must bear on those matters in which States may decide freely (internal and external affairs – the domain reservé of States) ...”,UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Romania,ROU,7.1 Does the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives include only internal affairs?,No,"“...depending on the situation, interference in the internal or external affairs ... may constitute a violation of the principle of non-intervention.”",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Romania,ROU,7.2 Does the prohibition proscribe intervention in a state’s external (foreign) affairs?,Yes,"“...depending on the situation, interference in the internal or external affairs ... may constitute a violation of the principle of non-intervention.”",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Romania,ROU,7.3 Is the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives referred to as domaine réservé?,Yes,“the act must bear on those matters in which States may decide freely (internal and external affairs – the domain reservé of States)...”,UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Romania,ROU,"7.4 Is the domaine réservé limited to matters involving the state’s choice of a political, economic, social, and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy?",Ambiguous,"“...interference in the internal or external affairs of Romania (that is interference which causes or may cause harm to Romania ’s economic, political, social and/ or cultural system)...”",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Romania,ROU,7.5 Does international law regulation over a matter of sovereign prerogative remove it from the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Romania,ROU,7.6 Is critical infrastructure part of the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Romania,ROU,7.7 Are elections part of the domaine réservé?,Ambiguous,“...the principle of prohibition of the intervention in the internal affairs of another State should be addressed (situations of tampering with the electoral processes in other States are relevant as a discussion under this principle).”,UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Romania,ROU,7.8 Is the operation of legislatures a part of the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Romania,ROU,7.9 Is financial system stability part of the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Romania,ROU,7.10 Are medical services part of the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Russia,RUS,1. Is the prohibition of non-intervention a norm of customary international law?,Ambiguous,"“Russia assumes that, for the present, the international community has reached consensus on the applicability of the universally accepted principles and norms of international law...” “These include, in particular, the principles of sovereign equality of States, non-use of force and threat of force, settlement of international disputes by peaceful means, non-interference into internal affairs of States...”",UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
