Singapore,SGP,1. Is the prohibition of non-intervention a norm of customary international law?,Yes,"5. Singapore affirms that the following key principles enshrined in the UN Charter apply in cyberspace as they do in the physical world, and are of fundamental importance to small States, such as Singapore: First, the principles of State sovereignty and sovereign equality of all States. Singapore’s position is that a cyber operation could, in certain circumstances, amount to a violation of sovereignty. Second, and as a corollary of the first, the principle of non-intervention in each other’s internal affairs. Singapore affirms that the principle of non-intervention in the internal affairs of other States applies to cyberspace.","Singapore, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 83, ¶ 5 (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Singapore,SGP,2. Is the scope of the principle of non-intervention uncertain?,Silent,,,,
Singapore,SGP,3. Does the prohibition of non-intervention apply to cyber-operations?,Yes,"5. Singapore affirms that the following key principles enshrined in the UN Charter apply in cyberspace as they do in the physical world, and are of fundamental importance to small States, such as Singapore: First, the principles of State sovereignty and sovereign equality of all States. Singapore’s position is that a cyber operation could, in certain circumstances, amount to a violation of sovereignty. Second, and as a corollary of the first, the principle of non-intervention in each other’s internal affairs. Singapore affirms that the principle of non-intervention in the internal affairs of other States applies to cyberspace.","Singapore, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 83, ¶ 5 (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Singapore,SGP,4. Is coercion a constituent element of prohibited intervention?,Yes,"5. In Singapore’s view, intervention necessarily involves an element of coercion.","Singapore, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 83, ¶ 5 (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Singapore,SGP,4.1 Is coercion limited to the employment of physical force?,No,"5. In Singapore’s view, intervention necessarily involves an element of coercion. As non-exhaustive examples, where there is interference in Singapore’s electoral processes through cyber means, or cyber-attacks against our infrastructure in an attempt to coerce our government to take or forbear a certain course of action on a matter ordinarily within its sovereign prerogative, these instances will constitute a violation of the principle of non-intervention.","Singapore, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 83, ¶ 5 (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Singapore,SGP,4.2 Can dictatorial demands or acts of compulsion or imposition constitute coercion?,Silent,,,,
Singapore,SGP,4.3 Does the deprivation of a State’s ability to control matters within its domaine réservé amount to coercion?,Silent,,,,
Singapore,SGP,4.4 Does coercion extend to any affirmative acts designed to deprive another state of its freedom of choice with regard to a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?,Silent,,,,
Singapore,SGP,4.5 Must there be a causal nexus between the responsible state’s actions/efforts and the effects on the target state’s internal or external affairs?,Silent,,,,
Singapore,SGP,4.6 Can attempts to coerce an outcome amount to prohibited intervention?,Yes,"5. In Singapore’s view, intervention necessarily involves an element of coercion. As non-exhaustive examples, where there is interference in Singapore’s electoral processes through cyber means, or cyber-attacks against our infrastructure in an attempt to coerce our government to take or forbear a certain course of action on a matter ordinarily within its sovereign prerogative, these instances will constitute a violation of the principle of non-intervention.","Singapore, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 83, ¶ 5 (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Singapore,SGP,4.7 Is intent a constituent element of coercion?,Silent,,,,
Singapore,SGP,"4.8 Must the action(s) constituting coercion be designed/intended to influence outcomes in, or conduct with respect to, a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?",Yes,"5. A prohibited intervention by one State against another must have a bearing on matters in which the victim State is permitted, by the principle of State sovereignty, to decide freely, including its choice of a political, economic, social and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy.","Singapore, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 83, ¶ 5 (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Singapore,SGP,4.9  Can coercion be indirect?,Ambiguous,,,,
Singapore,SGP,4.10 Must the target state have knowledge of the coercive cyber operation constituting the purported intervention?,Silent,,,,
