Switzerland,CHE,1. Is the prohibition of non-intervention a norm of customary international law?,Yes,“The principle of non-intervention is the corollary of the sovereign equality of all states (Art. 2 ¶ 1 UN Charter) and is considered customary international law.”,"Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace: Annex to UN GGE Switzerland, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 87, §3 (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Switzerland,CHE,2. Is the scope of the principle of non-intervention uncertain?,Silent,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,3. Does the prohibition of non-intervention apply to cyber-operations?,Yes,“The prohibition of intervention is also applicable to cyberspace.”,"Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace: Annex to UN GGE Switzerland, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 88, §3 (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Switzerland,CHE,4. Is coercion a constituent element of prohibited intervention?,Yes,"“In this context, intervention is understood to be the direct or indirect interference by one sovereign state in the internal or external affairs of another using coercive measures.”   “An infringement of sovereignty and a prohibited intervention are not the same. The latter must be coercive in nature, i.e. through its intervention a state seeks to cause another to act (or refrain from acting) in a way it would not otherwise.”","Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace: Annex to UN GGE Switzerland, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 87, §3 (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Switzerland,CHE,4.1 Is coercion limited to the employment of physical force?,No,"The distinction between exerting influence, which is permissible, and coercion, which is not, must be determined on a case-by-case basis. This is particularly true of economic coercion, which could be the case if a company that is systemically relevant was paralysed through a cyber operation. An assessment of whether the operation can be deemed coercive in nature, and thereby be in breach of the non-intervention principle, can only be made on a case-by-case basis.",position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace: Annex to UN GGE     UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions,https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Switzerland,CHE,4.2 Can dictatorial demands or acts of compulsion or imposition constitute coercion?,Silent,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,4.3 Does the deprivation of a State’s ability to control matters within its domaine réservé amount to coercion?,Silent,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,4.4 Does coercion extend to any affirmative acts designed to deprive another state of its freedom of choice with regard to a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?,Silent,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,4.5 Must there be a causal nexus between the responsible state’s actions/efforts and the effects on the target state’s internal or external affairs?,Yes,"“The latter must be coercive in nature, i.e. through its intervention a state seeks to cause another to act (or refrain from acting) in a way it would not otherwise.”","Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace: Annex to UN GGE Switzerland, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 87-88, §3 (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Switzerland,CHE,4.6 Can attempts to coerce an outcome amount to prohibited intervention?,Ambiguous,"“An infringement of sovereignty and a prohibited intervention are not the same. The latter must be coercive in nature, i.e. through its intervention a state seeks to cause another to act (or refrain from acting) in a way it would not otherwise.”","Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace: Annex to UN GGE Switzerland, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 87-88, §3 (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Switzerland,CHE,4.7 Is intent a constituent element of coercion?,Silent,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,"4.8 Must the action(s) constituting coercion be designed/intended to influence outcomes in, or conduct with respect to, a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?",Silent,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,4.9  Can coercion be indirect?,Yes,"“In this context, intervention is understood to be the direct or indirect interference by one sovereign state in the internal or external affairs of another using coercive measures.”","Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace: Annex to UN GGE Switzerland, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 87, §3 (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Switzerland,CHE,4.10 Must the target state have knowledge of the coercive cyber operation constituting the purported intervention?,Silent,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,5. Does intervention include forms of persuasion or propaganda?,Silent,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,6. Is there a de minimis exception to the prohibition?,Silent,,,,
Switzerland,CHE,"7. Must the intervention be directed at matters over which the victim state is permitted by the principle of sovereignty, to decide freely?",Yes,“...intervention is understood to be the direct or indirect interference by one sovereign state in the internal or external affairs of another using coercive measures. It covers those areas where the state has exclusive jurisdiction (known as domaine réservé).”,"Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace: Annex to UN GGE Switzerland, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 87, §3 (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Switzerland,CHE,7.1 Does the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives include only internal affairs?,No,"“In this context, intervention is understood to be the direct or indirect interference by one sovereign state in the internal or external affairs of another using coercive measures. It covers those areas where the state has exclusive jurisdiction (known as domaine réservé).”","Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace: Annex to UN GGE Switzerland, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 87, §3 (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
Switzerland,CHE,7.2 Does the prohibition proscribe intervention in a state’s external (foreign) affairs?,Yes,"“In this context, intervention is understood to be the direct or indirect interference by one sovereign state in the internal or external affairs of another using coercive measures. It covers those areas where the state has exclusive jurisdiction (known as domaine réservé).”","Switzerland's position paper on the application of international law in cyberspace: Annex to UN GGE Switzerland, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 87, §3 (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
