Thailand,THA,1. Is the prohibition of non-intervention a norm of customary international law?,Yes,"¶15. The prohibition of intervention is a fundamental principle of international law, firmly established in customary international law, and a direct corollary of the principle of sovereignty","Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, ¶15 (2025).",https://perma.cc/A5JV-UA3F,
Thailand,THA,2. Is the scope of the principle of non-intervention uncertain?,Yes,"¶16. The International Court of Justice (ICJ) affirmed this principle in the Case Concerning Military and Paramilitary Activities in and Against Nicaragua (Nicaragua v. United States of America), emphasizing that coercion constitutes the essence of an unlawful intervention. While the precise meaning of “coercion” remains under discussion among States, the ICJ clarified that direct or indirect coercive measures, including the threat or use of force, may constitute unlawful intervention.","Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, ¶16 (2025).",https://perma.cc/A5JV-UA3F,
Thailand,THA,3. Does the prohibition of non-intervention apply to cyber-operations?,Yes,¶17. Thailand affirms that the prohibition of intervention applies in full to cyberspace.,"Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 5, ¶17 (2025).",https://perma.cc/A5JV-UA3F,
Thailand,THA,4. Is coercion a constituent element of prohibited intervention?,Yes,"¶16. The International Court of Justice (ICJ) affirmed this principle in the Case Concerning Military and Paramilitary Activities in and Against Nicaragua (Nicaragua v. United States of America), emphasizing that coercion constitutes the essence of an unlawful intervention. While the precise meaning of “coercion” remains under discussion among States, the ICJ clarified that direct or indirect coercive measures, including the threat or use of force, may constitute unlawful intervention.","Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, ¶16 (2025).",https://perma.cc/A5JV-UA3F,
Thailand,THA,4.1 Is coercion limited to the employment of physical force?,No,"¶16. The 1970 Declaration on Principles of International Law Concerning Friendly Relations and Co-operation among States in accordance with the Charter of the United Nations further recognizes that coercion can also arise through non-forcible means, such as economic or political pressure aimed at compelling a State to alter its conduct.","Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, ¶16 (2025).",https://perma.cc/A5JV-UA3F,
Thailand,THA,4.2 Can dictatorial demands or acts of compulsion or imposition constitute coercion?,Yes,"¶16. The 1970 Declaration on Principles of International Law Concerning Friendly Relations and Co-operation among States in accordance with the Charter of the United Nations further recognizes that coercion can also arise through non-forcible means, such as economic or political pressure aimed at compelling a State to alter its conduct.","Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, ¶16 (2025).",https://perma.cc/A5JV-UA3F,
Thailand,THA,4.3 Does the deprivation of a State’s ability to control matters within its domaine réservé amount to coercion?,Yes,"¶18. Coercion may arise where a cyber operation undermines a State’s sovereign functions or impairs its ability to exercise its reserved domain (domaine réservé) freely, even without an explicit demand, demonstrable motive, or intention to compel.","Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 5, ¶18 (2025).",https://perma.cc/A5JV-UA3F,
Thailand,THA,4.4 Does coercion extend to any affirmative acts designed to deprive another state of its freedom of choice with regard to a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?,Yes,"¶18. Coercion may arise where a cyber operation undermines a State’s sovereign functions or impairs its ability to exercise its reserved domain (domaine réservé) freely, even without an explicit demand, demonstrable motive, or intention to compel.","Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 5, ¶18 (2025).",https://perma.cc/A5JV-UA3F,
Thailand,THA,4.5 Must there be a causal nexus between the responsible state’s actions/efforts and the effects on the target state’s internal or external affairs?,Silent,,,,
Thailand,THA,4.6 Can attempts to coerce an outcome amount to prohibited intervention?,Silent,,,,
Thailand,THA,4.7 Is intent a constituent element of coercion?,No,"¶18. Coercion may arise... even without an explicit demand, demonstrable motive, or intention to compel.","Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 5, ¶18 (2025)",https://perma.cc/A5JV-UA3F,
Thailand,THA,"4.8 Must the action(s) constituting coercion be designed/intended to influence outcomes in, or conduct with respect to, a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?",No,"¶18. Thailand affirms that coercion must be assessed on a case-by-case basis, taking into account the nature, scale, consequences, and context of a cyber operation. Coercion may arise where a cyber operation undermines a State’s sovereign functions or impairs its ability to exercise its reserved domain (domaine réservé) freely, even without an explicit demand, demonstrable motive, or intention to compel. The assessment should focus primarily on the practical effects and severity of the interference","Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 5, ¶18 (2025)",https://perma.cc/A5JV-UA3F,
Thailand,THA,4.9 Can coercion be indirect?,Yes,"¶16. … the ICJ clarified that direct or indirect coercive measures, including the threat or use of force, may constitute unlawful intervention","Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, ¶16 (2025)",https://perma.cc/A5JV-UA3F,
Thailand,THA,4.10 Must the target state have knowledge of the coercive cyber operation constituting the purported intervention?,Silent,,,,
Thailand,THA,5. Does intervention include forms of persuasion or propaganda?,Silent,,,,
Thailand,THA,6. Is there a de minimis exception to the prohibition?,Silent,,,,
Thailand,THA,"7. Must the intervention be directed at matters over which the victim state is permitted by the principle of sovereignty, to decide freely?",Yes,"¶15. …which obligate States to respect the sovereign rights of other States in matters within their exclusive jurisdiction, including political, economic, social, and foreign policy affairs ¶17. …It prohibits cyber operations that intervene with a State’s domaine réservé, particularly where such operations target sovereign governmental functions or essential public services. ¶18. Coercion may arise where a cyber operation undermines a State’s sovereign functions or impairs its ability to exercise its reserved domain (domaine réservé) freely,","Thailand, National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4-5, ¶¶ 15,17-18 (2025)",https://perma.cc/A5JV-UA3F,
Thailand,THA,7.1 Does the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives include only internal affairs?,No,"¶15. Articles 2(1) and 2(7) of the Charter of the United Nations and Article 2(2) of the ASEAN Charter, which obligate States to respect the sovereign rights of other States in matters within their exclusive jurisdiction, including political, economic, social, and foreign policy affairs","Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, ¶15 (2025)",https://perma.cc/A5JV-UA3F,
Thailand,THA,7.2 Does the prohibition proscribe intervention in a state’s external (foreign) affairs?,Yes,"¶15. Articles 2(1) and 2(7) of the Charter of the United Nations and Article 2(2) of the ASEAN Charter, which obligate States to respect the sovereign rights of other States in matters within their exclusive jurisdiction, including political, economic, social, and foreign policy affairs","Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 4, ¶15 (2025)",https://perma.cc/A5JV-UA3F,
Thailand,THA,7.3 Is the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives referred to as domaine réservé?,Yes,"¶17. Thailand affirms that the prohibition of intervention applies in full to cyberspace. It prohibits cyber operations that intervene with a State’s domaine réservé, particularly where such operations target sovereign governmental functions or essential public services","Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 5, ¶17 (2025)",https://perma.cc/A5JV-UA3F,
Thailand,THA,"7.4 Is the domaine réservé limited to matters involving the state’s choice of a political, economic, social, and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy?",No,"¶17.  It prohibits cyber operations that intervene with a State’s domaine réservé, particularly where such operations target sovereign governmental functions or essential public services.","Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 5, ¶17 (2025)",https://perma.cc/A5JV-UA3F,
Thailand,THA,7.5 Does international law regulation over a matter of sovereign prerogative remove it from the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Thailand,THA,7.6 Is critical infrastructure part of the domaine réservé?,Ambiguous,"¶17. … It prohibits cyber operations that intervene with a State’s domaine réservé, particularly where such operations target sovereign governmental functions or essential public services. ¶18. Coercion may arise where a cyber operation undermines a State’s sovereign functions or impairs its ability to exercise its reserved domain (domaine réservé) freely, even without an explicit demand, demonstrable motive, or intention to compel.","Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 5, ¶17-18 (2025)",https://perma.cc/A5JV-UA3F,
Thailand,THA,7.7 Are elections part of the domaine réservé?,Yes,"¶17. As non-exhaustive examples, coercive cyber operations that significantly impair Thailand’s capacity to conduct elections, maintain public administration, deliver essential services, or uphold internal security will constitute unlawful intervention under international law","Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 5, ¶17 (2025)",https://perma.cc/A5JV-UA3F,
Thailand,THA,7.8 Is the operation of legislatures a part of the domaine réservé?,Yes,"¶ It prohibits cyber operations that intervene with a State’s domaine réservé, particularly where such operations target sovereign governmental functions or essential public services","Thailand’s National Position on the Application of International Law in Cyberspace, pg. 5, ¶17 (2025)",https://perma.cc/A5JV-UA3F,
Thailand,THA,7.9 Is financial system stability part of the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
Thailand,THA,7.10 Are medical services part of the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
United Kingdom,GBR,1. Is the prohibition of non-intervention a norm of customary international law?,Yes,"“The rule on non-intervention. Turning now to the law, one of the rules of customary international law which is of particular importance in this area is the rule on non-intervention. Customary international law is the general practice of states accepted as law.” “Below the threshold of the threat or use of force, the customary international law rule prohibiting interventions in the domestic affairs of States applies to States’ operations in cyberspace as it does to their other activities.”","Attorney General Suella Braverman: International Law in Future Frontiers , pg. 6 (2022). United Kingdom, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 116, ¶ 8 (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
United Kingdom,GBR,2. Is the scope of the principle of non-intervention uncertain?,No,"“The UK’s position is that the rule on non-intervention provides a clearly established basis in international law for assessing the legality of state conduct in cyberspace during peacetime.” “As the UK has noted previously, while the precise boundaries of this rule continue to be the subject of on-going debate, it provides a clearly established basis in international law for assessing the legality of State conduct.” “Through the principle of non-intervention, it is clear that the international community has set a boundary at which interference in another state’s sovereign freedoms is considered internationally wrongful and as such, in breach of international law, giving rise to the right to take action which may otherwise be unlawful in response. As I have already mentioned, the precise parameters of this principle remain the subject of ongoing debate in the international law community, but a further contested are amongst those engaged in the application of international law to cyber space is the regulation of activities that fall below the threshold of a prohibited intervention, but nonetheless may be perceived as affecting the territorial sovereignty of another state without that state’s prior consent.”","Attorney General Suella Braverman: International Law in Future Frontiers , pg. 6 (2022) United Kingdom, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 116, ¶ 9  (2021) Attorney General Jeremy Wright: Cyber and International Law in the 21st Century , pg. 7 (2018)",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/United%20Kingdom/United%20Kingdom_2018%20Speech.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=dUKWnM,
United Kingdom,GBR,3. Does the prohibition of non-intervention apply to cyber-operations?,Yes,"“This rule [non-intervention] is particularly important in cyberspace for two main reasons. ¶9.“Thus the use of hostile cyber operations to manipulate the electoral system in another State to alter the results of an election, to undermine the stability of another State’s financial system or to target the essential medical services of another State could all, depending on the circumstances, be in violation of the international law prohibition on intervention.” “First, there is the rule prohibiting interventions in the domestic affairs of states both under Article 2(7) of the Charter and in customary international law. This prohibition means that any activity in cyber space which reaches the level of such an intervention is unlawful.”","Attorney General Suella Braverman: International Law in Future Frontiers , pg. 6 (2022). United Kingdom, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 116-117, ¶9 (2021). Attorney General Jeremy Wright: Cyber and International Law in the 21st Century , pg. 5 (2018).",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/United%20Kingdom/United%20Kingdom_2018%20Speech.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=dUKWnM,
United Kingdom,GBR,4. Is coercion a constituent element of prohibited intervention?,Yes,"“A core element of the non-intervention rule is that the offensive behaviour must be coercive.” ¶8. “As set out by the International Court of Justice in its judgment in the Nicaragua case, the purpose of the rule on non-intervention is to ensure that all States remain free from external coercive intervention in matters affecting a State’s powers, which are at the heart of a State’s sovereignty such as the freedom to choose its own political, social, economic and cultural system.” “As set out by the International Court of Justice in its judgment in the Nicaragua case, the purpose of this principle is to ensure that all states remain free from external, coercive intervention in the matters of government which are at the heart of a state’s sovereignty, such as the freedom to choose its own political, social, economic and cultural system.”","Attorney General Suella Braverman: International Law in Future Frontiers , pg. 7 (2022). United Kingdom, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 116, ¶ 8  (2021). Attorney General Jeremy Wright: Cyber and International Law in the 21st Century , pg. 7 (2018).",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/United%20Kingdom/United%20Kingdom_2018%20Speech.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=dUKWnM,
United Kingdom,GBR,4.1 Is coercion limited to the employment of physical force?,No,"“Imagine, for example, a cyber operation to delay another state’s election or to prevent it from distributing tax revenues to fund essential services. To my mind, these are certainly forms of coercion.” ¶9. “Thus the use of hostile cyber operations to manipulate the electoral system in another State to alter the results of an election, to undermine the stability of another State’s financial system or to target the essential medical services of another State could all, depending on the circumstances, be in violation of the international law prohibition on intervention.” “But the practical application of the principle in this context would be the use by a hostile state of cyber operations to manipulate the electoral system to alter the results of an election in another state, intervention in the fundamental operation of Parliament, or in the stability of our financial system. Such acts must surely be a breach of the prohibition on intervention in the domestic affairs of states.”","Attorney General Suella Braverman: International Law in Future Frontiers United Kingdom, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 116-17, ¶9 (2021). Attorney General Jeremy Wright: Cyber and International Law in the 21st Century , pg. 6 (2018).",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/United%20Kingdom/United%20Kingdom_2018%20Speech.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=dUKWnM,
United Kingdom,GBR,4.2 Can dictatorial demands or acts of compulsion or imposition constitute coercion?,Yes,"“But I want to be clear that coercion can be broader than this. In essence, an intervention in the affairs of another state will be unlawful if it is forcible, dictatorial or otherwise coercive, depriving a state of its freedom of control over matters, which it is permitted to decide freely by the principle of state sovereignty.”",Attorney General Suella Braverman: International Law in Future Frontiers,https://perma.cc/69JF-VV9Y,
United Kingdom,GBR,4.3 Does the deprivation of a State’s ability to control matters within its domaine réservé amount to coercion?,Yes,"“But I want to be clear that coercion can be broader than this. In essence, an intervention in the affairs of another state will be unlawful if it is forcible, dictatorial or otherwise coercive, depriving a state of its freedom of control over matters, which it is permitted to decide freely by the principle of state sovereignty.”",Attorney General Suella Braverman: International Law in Future Frontiers,https://perma.cc/69JF-VV9Y,
