United Kingdom,GBR,"7. Must the intervention be directed at matters over which the victim state is permitted by the principle of sovereignty, to decide freely?",Yes,"“A prohibited intervention must accordingly be one bearing on matters in which each state is permitted, by the principle of state sovereignty, to decide freely.” ¶8. “As set out by the International Court of Justice in its judgment in the Nicaragua case, the purpose of the rule on non-intervention is to ensure that all States remain free from external coercive intervention in matters affecting a State’s powers, which are at the heart of a State’s sovereignty such as the freedom to choose its own political, social, economic and cultural system.” “As set out by the International Court of Justice in its judgment in the Nicaragua case, the purpose of the rule on non-intervention is to ensure that all States remain free from external coercive intervention in matters affecting a State’s powers, which are at the heart of a State’s sovereignty such as the freedom to choose its own political, social, economic and cultural system.”","Attorney General Suella Braverman: International Law in Future Frontiers United Kingdom, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 116, ¶8 (2021). Attorney General Jeremy Wright: Cyber and International Law in the 21st Century , pg. 6 (2018).",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/United%20Kingdom/United%20Kingdom_2018%20Speech.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=dUKWnM,
United Kingdom,GBR,7.1 Does the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives include only internal affairs?,No,"“According to the court in that case, all states, or groups of states, are forbidden from intervening, ‘directly or indirectly in internal or external affairs of other states.’”",Attorney General Suella Braverman: International Law in Future Frontiers,https://perma.cc/69JF-VV9Y,
United Kingdom,GBR,7.2 Does the prohibition proscribe intervention in a state’s external (foreign) affairs?,Yes,"“According to the court in that case, all states, or groups of states, are forbidden from intervening, ‘directly or indirectly in internal or external affairs of other states.’”",Attorney General Suella Braverman: International Law in Future Frontiers,https://perma.cc/69JF-VV9Y,
United Kingdom,GBR,7.3 Is the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives referred to as domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
United Kingdom,GBR,"7.4 Is the domaine réservé limited to matters involving the state’s choice of a political, economic, social, and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy?",Ambiguous,"“A prohibited intervention must accordingly be one bearing on matters in which each state is permitted, by the principle of state sovereignty, to decide freely.” ¶8. “As set out by the International Court of Justice in its judgment in the Nicaragua case, the purpose of the rule on non-intervention is to ensure that all States remain free from external coercive intervention in matters affecting a State’s powers, which are at the heart of a State’s sovereignty such as the freedom to choose its own political, social, economic and cultural system.” “As set out by the International Court of Justice in its judgment in the Nicaragua case, the purpose of the rule on non-intervention is to ensure that all States remain free from external coercive intervention in matters affecting a State’s powers, which are at the heart of a State’s sovereignty such as the freedom to choose its own political, social, economic and cultural system.”","Attorney General Suella Braverman: International Law in Future Frontiers United Kingdom, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 116, ¶8 (2021). Attorney General Jeremy Wright: Cyber and International Law in the 21st Century , pg. 6 (2018).",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/United%20Kingdom/United%20Kingdom_2018%20Speech.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=dUKWnM,
United Kingdom,GBR,7.5 Does international law regulation over a matter of sovereign prerogative remove it from the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
United Kingdom,GBR,7.6 Is critical infrastructure part of the domaine réservé?,Yes,“Ensuring the provision of essential medical services and secure and reliable energy supply to a population are sovereign functions of a state.”,Attorney General Suella Braverman: International Law in Future Frontiers,https://perma.cc/69JF-VV9Y,
United Kingdom,GBR,7.7 Are elections part of the domaine réservé?,Yes,"“However, covert cyber operations by a foreign state, which coercively interfere with free and fair elections, or electoral processes, would constitute a prohibited intervention”",Attorney General Suella Braverman: International Law in Future Frontiers,https://perma.cc/69JF-VV9Y,
United Kingdom,GBR,7.8 Is the operation of legislatures a part of the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
United Kingdom,GBR,7.9 Is financial system stability part of the domaine réservé?,Yes,"“Turning to economic stability, covert cyber operations by a foreign state that coercively interfere with a state’s freedom to manage its domestic economy or to ensure provision of domestic financial services crucial to the state’s financial system, would also breach the rule on non-intervention. Such cyber operations could include disruption to the networks controlling a state’s fundamental ability to conduct monetary policy or to raise and distribute revenue, for instance through taxation, or disruption to systems, which support lending, saving and insurance across the economy...” ¶9. “Thus the use of hostile cyber operations to manipulate the electoral system in another State to alter the results of an election, to undermine the stability of another State’s financial system or to target the essential medical services of another State could all, depending on the circumstances, be in violation of the international law prohibition on intervention.” “But the practical application of the principle in this context would be the use by a hostile state of cyber operations to manipulate the electoral system to alter the results of an election in another state, intervention in the fundamental operation of Parliament, or in the stability of our financial system. Such acts must surely be a breach of the prohibition on intervention in the domestic affairs of states.”","Attorney General Suella Braverman: International Law in Future Frontiers United Kingdom, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 116-17, ¶9 (2021). Attorney General Jeremy Wright: Cyber and International Law in the 21st Century",https://perma.cc/U39M-962P,
United Kingdom,GBR,7.10 Are medical services part of the domaine réservé?,Yes,"“Ensuring the provision of essential medical services and secure and reliable energy supply to a population are sovereign functions of a state.” ¶9. “Thus the use of hostile cyber operations to manipulate the electoral system in another State to alter the results of an election, to undermine the stability of another State’s financial system or to target the essential medical services of another State could all, depending on the circumstances, be in violation of the international law prohibition on intervention.”","Attorney General Suella Braverman: International Law in Future Frontiers United Kingdom, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 116-17, ¶ 9 (2021)",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
United States,USA,1. Is the prohibition of non-intervention a norm of customary international law?,Yes,"“I'll begin with the customary international law rule of prohibited intervention, a concept that is also reflected in certain treaties that the United States has joined.” -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- “Among other international legal principles, the 2015 GGE report acknowledges the principle of non-intervention in the internal affairs of other States. As articulated by the International Court of Justice (ICJ) in its judgment on the merits in the Nicaragua Case, this rule of customary international law forbids States from engaging in coercive action that bears on a matter that each State is entitled, by the principle of State sovereignty, to decide freely, such as the choice of a political, economic, social, and cultural system.” --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- “Relatedly, consider the challenges we face in clarifying the international law prohibition on unlawful intervention. As articulated by the International Court of Justice (ICJ) in its judgment on the merits in the Nicaragua Case, this rule of customary international law forbids States from engaging in coercive action that bears on a matter that each State is entitled, by the principle of State sovereignty, to decide freely, such as the choice of a political, economic, social, and cultural system. This is generally viewed as a relatively narrow rule of customary international law...”","Hon. Caroline Krass, DOD General Counsel Remarks at U.S. Cyber Command Legal Conference , §III(a) (2023). United States, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 139-40, §III(A) (2021). Brian J. Egan, International Law and Stability in Cyberspace",https://perma.cc/8RU4-AXL2,
United States,USA,2. Is the scope of the principle of non-intervention uncertain?,Yes,"A few questions emerge from these statements, including: The ICJ’s articulation of prohibited intervention does not expressly include a territorial element. Does a “deprivation of control” theory of coercion for cyber activities implicitly depend on the existence of effects within a State’s territory?  If so, how would nonintervention be different from the rule of territorial sovereignty articulated by many States?  If not, is there another limiting principle that would distinguish prohibited intervention from lawful forms of pressure?  How does intent fit into the analysis of prohibited intervention? Assuming it is relevant, how can intent be established with respect to cyber activities?  Is there a customary international law basis for finding a prohibited intervention where the attempt to interfere failed to achieve the intended results? --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- “Although the scope of prohibited intervention is generally understood to be relatively narrow, certain peacetime activities of States in cyberspace could run afoul of this high threshold.” 
 -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- 
For increased transparency, States need to do more work to clarify how the international law on non-intervention applies to States’ activities in cyberspace.","Richard C. Visek, International Law and Cyberspace: Building Consensus , pg. 12 (2024). Hon. Caroline Krass, DOD General Counsel Remarks at U.S. Cyber Command Legal Conference , pg. 7, § III(a) (2023). Brian J. Egan, International Law and Stability in Cyberspace",https://perma.cc/8RU4-AXL2,
United States,USA,3. Does the prohibition of non-intervention apply to cyber-operations?,Yes,"“The United States has previously said, for example, that a cyber operation by a state that interferes with another country's ability to hold an election, or that manipulates another country's election results, would violate the rule of non-intervention.” --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- “…is generally viewed as a relatively narrow rule of customary international law, but States’ cyber activities could run afoul of this Prohibition.""  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- “For example, a cyber operation by a State that interferes with another country’s ability to hold an election or that manipulates another country’s election results would be a clear violation of the rule of non-intervention.”","Hon. Caroline Krass, DOD General Counsel Remarks at U.S. Cyber Command Legal Conference , pg. 7, § III(a) (2023). United States, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 140, § III(A) (2021). Brian J. Egan, International Law and Stability in Cyberspace",https://perma.cc/8RU4-AXL2,
United States,USA,4. Is coercion a constituent element of prohibited intervention?,Yes,"“...the rule of prohibited intervention forbids States from engaging in coercive action that bears on a matter that each State is entitled, by the principle of State sovereignty, to decide freely.” ... “It is widely recognized that prohibited intervention includes two elements: (1) the action must interfere in the matters the targeted State is permitted to decide freely under the principle of sovereignty; and (2) it must be coercive.  However, the precise meaning and contours of these elements are not well-defined — and cyberspace magnifies these already existing uncertainties.  Working with our Allies and partners, and appreciating contributions from civil society and academia, to advance the understanding of these fundamental concepts can contribute to the broader goal of a shared and more nuanced view of how international law applies in cyberspace.” --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- Among other international legal principles, the 2015 GGE report acknowledges the principle of non-intervention in the internal affairs of other States. As articulated by the International Court of Justice (ICJ) in its judgment on the merits in the Nicaragua Case, this rule of customary international law forbids States from engaging in coercive action that bears on a matter that each State is entitled, by the principle of State sovereignty, to decide freely, such as the choice of a political, economic, social, and cultural system.” ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
“...this rule of customary international law forbids States from engaging in coercive action that bears on a matter that each State is entitled, by the principle of State sovereignty, to decide freely, such as the choice of a political, economic, social, and cultural system.”","Hon. Caroline Krass, DOD General Counsel Remarks at U.S. Cyber Command Legal Conference , pg. 7, § III(a) (2023). United States, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 139-40, §III(A) (2021). Brian J. Egan, International Law and Stability in Cyberspace",https://perma.cc/8RU4-AXL2,
United States,USA,4.1 Is coercion limited to the employment of physical force?,No,"few questions emerge from these statements, including: The ICJ’s articulation of prohibited intervention does not expressly include a territorial element. Does a “deprivation of control” theory of coercion for cyber activities implicitly depend on the existence of effects within a State’s territory? If so, how would nonintervention be different from the rule of territorial sovereignty articulated by many States?  If not, is there another limiting principle that would distinguish prohibited intervention from lawful forms of pressure? -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- “For example, a cyber operation by a State that interferes with another country’s ability to hold an election or that manipulates another country’s election results would be a clear violation of the rule of non-intervention.”","Richard C. Visek, International Law and Cyberspace: Building Consensus United States, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 140, §III(A) (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
