United States,USA,4.3 Does the deprivation of a State’s ability to control matters within its domaine réservé amount to coercion?,Silent,,,,
United States,USA,4.4 Does coercion extend to any affirmative acts designed to deprive another state of its freedom of choice with regard to a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?,Silent,,,,
United States,USA,4.5 Must there be a causal nexus between the responsible state’s actions/efforts and the effects on the target state’s internal or external affairs?,Silent,,,,
United States,USA,4.6 Can attempts to coerce an outcome amount to prohibited intervention?,yes,"“...the rule of prohibited intervention forbids States from engaging in coercive action that bears on a matter that each State is entitled, by the principle of State sovereignty, to decide freely.” -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- “...this rule of customary international law forbids States from engaging in coercive action that bears on a matter that each State is entitled, by the principle of State sovereignty, to decide freely, such as the choice of a political, economic, social, and cultural system”","Hon. Caroline Krass, DOD General Counsel Remarks at U.S. Cyber Command Legal Conference Brian J. Egan, International Law and Stability in Cyberspace",https://perma.cc/8RU4-AXL2,
United States,USA,4.7 Is intent a constituent element of coercion?,Silent,,,,
United States,USA,"4.8 Must the action(s) constituting coercion be designed/intended to influence outcomes in, or conduct with respect to, a matter of protected sovereign prerogative?",Silent,,,,
United States,USA,4.9   Can coercion be indirect?,Silent,,,,
United States,USA,4.10 Must the target state have knowledge of the coercive cyber operation constituting the purported intervention?,Silent,,,,
United States,USA,5. Does intervention include forms of persuasion or propaganda?,Ambiguous,"A couple of States suggest that cyber-enabled propaganda or disinformation could play a role in prohibited intervention, but for these States, it seems there would need to be concrete consequences caused by that information that affected the ability of the State to act freely. ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- “For example, a cyber operation by a State that interferes with another country’s ability to hold an election or that manipulates another country’s election results would be a clear violation of the rule of non-intervention.”","Richard C. Visek, International Law and Cyberspace: Building Consensus , pg. 11 (2024). United States, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 140, §III(A) (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
United States,USA,6. Is there a de minimis exception to the prohibition?,yes,"Although the scope of prohibited intervention is generally understood to be relatively narrow, certain peacetime activities of States in cyberspace could run afoul of this high threshold.   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- In certain circumstances, one State’s non-consensual cyber operation in another State’s territory, even if it falls below the threshold of a use of force or nonintervention, could also violate international law. ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- “As an initial matter, remote cyber operations involving computers or other networked devices located on another State’s territory do not constitute a per se violation of international law. In other words, there is no absolute prohibition on such operations as a matter of international law. This is perhaps most clear where such activities in another State’s territory have no effects or de minimis effects.”","Hon. Caroline Krass, DOD General Counsel Remarks at U.S. Cyber Command Legal Conference , pg. 7, § III(a) (2023). United States, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 140, § III(A) (2021). Brian J. Egan, International Law and Stability in Cyberspace",https://perma.cc/8RU4-AXL2,
United States,USA,"7. Must the intervention be directed at matters over which the victim state is permitted by the principle of sovereignty, to decide freely?",Yes,"In its merits judgment in the Nicaragua v. United States case, the ICJ singled out “the choice of a political, economic, social and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy” as among those things that international law provides that States must be able to decide freely. The United States and many others have cited the ICJ’s articulation of this rule in affirming their belief that a State’s cyber activities can constitute a prohibited intervention --- As articulated by the International Court of Justice (ICJ) in its judgment on the merits in the Nicaragua Case, this rule of customary international law forbids States from engaging in coercive action that bears on a matter that each State is entitled, by the principle of State sovereignty, to decide freely, such as the choice of a political, economic, social, and cultural system. This is generally viewed as a relatively narrow rule of customary international law...” --- “As articulated by the International Court of Justice (ICJ) in its judgment on the merits in the Nicaragua Case, this rule [Non-intervention] of customary international law forbids States from engaging in coercive action that bears on a matter that each State is entitled, by the principle of State sovereignty, to decide freely, such as the choice of a political, economic, social, and cultural system. This is generally viewed as a relatively narrow rule of customary international law, but States’ cyber activities could run afoul of this Prohibition.”","Richard C. Visek, International Law and Cyberspace: Building Consensus United States, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 139-40, § III(A) (2021). Brian J. Egan, International Law and Stability in Cyberspace",https://perma.cc/8RU4-AXL2,
United States,USA,7.1 Does the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives include only internal affairs?,Ambiguous,"“Among other international legal principles, the 2015 GGE report acknowledges the principle of non-intervention in the internal affairs of other States.”","United States, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 139, §III(A) (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
United States,USA,7.2 Does the prohibition proscribe intervention in a state’s external (foreign) affairs?,Silent,,,,
United States,USA,7.3 Is the zone of protected sovereign prerogatives referred to as domaine réservé?,No,"“Many commentators refer to these matters as a State's ""domaine réservé"" — or ""reserved domain"" in English. ...the United States does not publicly use that phrase in formal writings...”","Hon. Caroline Krass, DOD General Counsel Remarks at U.S. Cyber Command Legal Conference , pg. 7, §III(a)(i) (2023).",https://tuprd-my.sharepoint.com/:b:/r/personal/tup46628_temple_edu/Documents/iLIT%20Projects/iLIT%20RA%20Files/Working%20Folders%20%5BActive%20Projects%5D/Cyber/National%20Cyber%20Statements/Source%20Materials/National%20Statements/United%20States/United%20States_2023_DOD%20General%20Counsel%20Remarks%20at%20U.S.%20Cyber%20Command%20Legal%20Conference%20_%20U.S.%20Department%20of%20Defense%20_%20Speech.pdf?csf=1&web=1&e=C0T3x1,
United States,USA,"7.4 Is the domaine réservé limited to matters involving the state’s choice of a political, economic, social, and cultural system, and the formulation of foreign policy?",Silent,,,,
United States,USA,7.5 Does international law regulation over a matter of sovereign prerogative remove it from the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
United States,USA,7.6 Is critical infrastructure part of the domaine réservé?,Ambiguous,"The United States in 2021 identified manipulation of a State's election results, or coercive interference in a State's ability to hold an election or to protect the health of its population, as potentially constituting violations of the rule.  In this respect, certain aspects of a State's provision of healthcare could be part of its domaine réservé, whether administered by the State or predominantly through the private sector. --- “In addition to the potential physical damage that a cyber activity may cause, such as death or injury that may result from effects on critical infrastructure, parties must assess the potential effects of a cyber attack on civilian objects that are not military objectives, such as private, civilian computers that hold no military significance but may be networked to military objectives.”","Hon. Caroline Krass, DOD General Counsel Remarks at U.S. Cyber Command Legal Conference , pg. 8, §III(a)(i) (2023). United States, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 138, § II(B) (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
United States,USA,7.7 Are elections part of the domaine réservé?,Yes,"The United States in 2021 identified manipulation of a State's election results, or coercive interference in a State's ability to hold an election or to protect the health of its population, as potentially constituting violations of the rule. ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ “For example, a cyber operation by a State that interferes with another country’s ability to hold an election or that manipulates another country’s election results would be a clear violation of the rule of non-intervention.”","Hon. Caroline Krass, DOD General Counsel Remarks at U.S. Cyber Command Legal Conference , pg. 8, §III(a)(i) (2023). United States, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 140, §III(A) (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
United States,USA,7.8 Is the operation of legislatures a part of the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
United States,USA,7.9 Is financial system stability part of the domaine réservé?,Silent,,,,
United States,USA,7.10 Are medical services part of the domaine réservé?,Ambiguous,"“In this respect, certain aspects of a State's provision of healthcare could be part of its domaine réservé, whether administered by the State or predominantly through the private sector.” -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- Further, a cyber operation that attempts to interfere coercively with a State’s ability to protect the health of its population--for example, through vaccine research or running cyber-controlled ventilators within its territories during a pandemic--could be considered a violation of the rule of non-intervention.","Hon. Caroline Krass, DOD General Counsel Remarks at U.S. Cyber Command Legal Conference , pg. 8-9, §III(a)(i) (2023). United States, UNGA Official Compendium of Voluntary National Contributions, pg. 140, §III(A) (2021).",https://perma.cc/UD7M-5NA7,
